\par\par \b \uc2  BOND MARKETS - Gilts slide after dlr hit by Fed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Clelia Oziel \par \uc2 916 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 15:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 17 (Reuters) - Gilts skidded on rate hike fears on Wednesday as U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar hit bonds across the board, breaking a euphoric flight-to-quality that had taken prices to historic highs, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , confirmed by the U.S. Treasury, pushed the U.S. unit back to 138.60/70 yen by 1245 GMT from levels above 142.20. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading above 146.00 earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  "There is a general selloff of bond products simply on the back of dollar weakness, and some support coming into the equity market," said Olivier Bonnefoy, institutional broker at Fimat in London. \par \par \uc2  "The negative correction that was built up between bonds and equities is no longer needed and that is also adding a lot of pressure." \par \par \uc2  "People have started to use the European products as a hedge and have started to sell heavily, knocking out a great deal of technical supports," said Bonnefoy. \par \par \uc2  The 30-year benchmark Treasury bond shed eight basis points from the yield to 5.75 percent, with the price sliding to 105-09 from the prior U.S. close of 106-20. \par \par \uc2  Gilts, reeling under a spate of strong data this week and hawkish remarks from the Bank of England's top monetary policy officials, fell more than a point on the day before partially paring losses. \par \par \uc2  The sell off left gilts sharply behind Bunds, with the 10-year yield spread widening to 113 basis points from the 103 close on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  T-bond futures were also off more than one full point with the key September contract down 1-12/32 to 122-00, where they looked set to put up a fierce struggle to hold above a crucial ascending channel. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts were quick to brush aside the potential longer term impact from the dollar's slide. \par \par \uc2  "We consider this as a blip, it doesn't change the fundamental picture," said one strategist at a leading U.S. bank. "Japan is still in trouble and we're still bearish on emerging markets generally and therefore bullish on bonds." \par \par \uc2  But Stephen Lewis, chief economist at London Bond Broking, said the dollar's retreat could be a psychological turning point for the bond markets. \par \par \uc2  "Perhaps by the time we get back to worrying about Asia, domestic fundamentals in the U.S. are not going to be looking so good," said Lewis. "So the flight into quality will not be able to override the evidence of rising inflation and still strong economic growth." \par \par \uc2  Later, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly in the dollar/yen with the U.S. Fed. Dealers have estimated that the two sold $2 billion during the U.S. morning session. \par \par \uc2  Lewis said investors should stay clear of long maturity bonds, at least until the dust settles. \par \par \uc2  "Cash looks very good because I don't think the adjustment in the bond markets has run its course yet," he said. "If one had to be in the bond market, then one would stay at the very short end." \par \par \uc2  Gilts may particularly remain a sore point with the latest rise in inflation and average earnings suggesting investors will continue to fret about possible rate rises after a 0.25 point hike two weeks ago that brought the repo rate to 7.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  "One could easily justify on the basis of the data that we've seen a move by the 10-year yield to well above six percent," said Lewis pointing at the current 5.84 percent yield. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Norwegian bonds struggled under fresh evidence of an overheating economy as a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  report stoked market speculation that it was poised to push up interest rates for the third time this year. \par \par \uc2  The Norges Bank urged for a tighter fiscal policy to control high wage growth, which has continued despite sharply lower oil prices. But the 10-year yield spread to Bunds still narrowed to 65 basis points, from 67 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  This is how major world bond markets looked at 1410 GMT on \par \par \uc2  Wednesday. \par \par \uc2      UNITED STATES - \par \par \uc2   Futures: September T-bonds at 122-11/32, down 1-1/32 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab     September Eurodollars at 94.285, down 0.040\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Cash:    Long bond at down 1-4/32 at 105-17/32, yld 5.73\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     GERMANY -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Futures: September Bunds on LIFFE at 107.94, down 0.36\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab     September Euromarks at 96.260, down 0.015\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Cash:    10-year Bund down 0.29 at 103.18, yld 4.82 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRITAIN -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Futures: September Long gilts on LIFFE at 108.78, down 0.85\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab     September Short sterling at 92.140, down 0.060\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Cash:    10-year gilt down 2-6/32 at 124-2/32, yld 5.85\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     FRANCE -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Futures: September Notionnel on MATIF at 104.12, down 0.36\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab     September PIBOR at 96.265, down 0.010\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Cash:    10-year bond (OAT) down 0.36 at 102.82, yld 4.88\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     ITALY 