\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's 'Mr Yen' likely to keep post - media. \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 01:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 18 (Reuters) - Japan's "Mr Yen", Eisuke Sakakibara, looks set to keep his job as top financial diplomat, Japanese media reported on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The mass circulation daily Sankei Shimbun said Sakakibara would retain his position after annual personnel reshuffles, expected to be announced soon. The Nihon Keizai Shimbun financial daily said final decisions were close at hand and Sakakibara was likely to stay. \par \par \uc2  Speculation had mounted that Sakakibara - once known for his ability to sway \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with his comments - might be ousted from his post as vice finance minister for international affairs in annual personnel reshuffles. \par \par \uc2  Some ruling politicians have made clear they want the controversial Sakakibara to go, and on Wednesday whispers rustled in financial markets that the decision was at hand. \par \par \uc2  Job changes announced late in the day, however, made no reference to Sakakibara's post. \par \par \uc2  Ousting Sakakibara now could be considered ill-timed, given that he is expected to meet his U.S. counterpart later this week for financial crisis talks, just on the heels of the first U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the yen in almost six years. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers arrives in Tokyo on Thursday and the two men will also take part in a joint meeting on Saturday with other officials of the Group of Seven industrial countries and the Manila Framework Group set up to deal with Asian issues. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the United States - in a dramatic shift from its hands off stance - joined \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to rescue the battered yen. \par \par \uc2  The outspoken Sakakibara won plaudits and his "Mr Yen" sobriquet when he helped engineer a reversal of the yen's rise to a post-war peak of above 80 to the dollar in 1995. \par \par \uc2  But his influence over \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had appeared to wane since then, as Japan's pledges of "decisive" action to stem the yen's decline have failed to move markets. \par \par \uc2  But on Thursday morning, the yen was trading at just under 137 to the dollar, buoyed by the first joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the yen in more than six years. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02pcc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
