\par\par \b \uc2  TOKYO STOCKS - Factors to watch - June 18. \b0\par\par\uc2 365 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 01:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 18 (Reuters) - The following are some of the main factors that may influence stock trading in Tokyo on Thursday: \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks are expected to bounce back after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to halt the yen's decline, which has prompted fears of another financial crisis in Asia, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  They expect the key Nikkei average to recover above 15,000. The market will focus on whether the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to buy the yen against the dollar and whether the yen's bearish trend has changed. \par \par \uc2  OUTLOOK \par \par \uc2  *"The Nikkei 225 will stage a sharp rebound today, but how far it goes depends on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said Yukio Takahashi, manager at Wako Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  *Brokers said stock average could reach 15,400, the level the Nikkei 225 futures in Chicago briefly touched overnight. \par \par \uc2  *"Since we had not expected joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it will turn market sentiment brighter. But we want to see a little more how \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  move in Tokyo," said a broker at a Japanese securities house. \par \par \uc2  *Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto said in statement on Wednesday that he and U.S. President Bill Clinton have agreed on need for strong yen. \par \par \uc2  *Market will also focus on visit by U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers to Japan expected later Thursday. Summers also due to attend meeting of deputy finance ministers from G7 (Group of Seven) industrial nations later this week. \par \par \uc2  MARKET CLOSINGS ON WEDNESDAY \par \par \uc2  *Nikkei 225 average ended off 5.00 points, or 0.03 percent, at 14,715.38. \par \par \uc2  *Nikkei September futures rose 140 points to 14,800. \par \par \uc2  *Nikkei 225 September futures traded in Chicago rose 565 points to close at 15,370. \par \par \uc2  *Dow Jones industrial average rose 164.17 points, or 1.89 percent, to end at 8,829.46. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar ended in New York at 136.90/00 yen. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS TO WATCH \par \par \uc2  *Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan Ltd (LTCB) , after bank called emergency news conference on Wednesday to deny market rumours about its financial health. ((Tokyo Equities Desk +81-3 3432 9404 tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02pbm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
