\par\par \b \uc2  US/ \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  said planned "days ago". \b0\par\par\uc2  By Isabelle Clary \par \uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 23:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 17 (Reuters) - U.S. and Japanese officials decided "days ago" to jointly act in support of the yen after Japan indicated a commitment to dealing with fundamental issues affecting its banking system and economy, a U.S. official, a foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official and a private expert said. \par \par \uc2  The decision was taken as part of a plan for Japan to take measures to restore the health of its banking system, they also said. \par \par \uc2  "An \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  like that is not decided overnight. It was decided days ago," a senior U.S. official told Reuters while commenting on the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by U.S. and Japanese authorities to support the yen beginning at 0800 EDT/1200 GMT on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent dollar/yen tumbling from 143.22 before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 136.80 in late New York trading. \par \par \uc2  "The statements are very clear about their intentions. In a case like this, the two sides go over the fine prints of their statements countless times before moving," the source added. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto, who spoke to U.S. President Bill Clinton late on Tuesday, issued a statement after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The government of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will expeditiously restructure the financial system, including the prompt disposal of bad assets," Hashimoto said in his statement. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at $5 billion to $6 billion. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. official, while noting that Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "very large," pointed out "\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not the solution to all ills. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  must deal with the fundamentals of their problems." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said in a statement the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurred "in the context of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plan to strengthen its economy." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Deputy Secretary Lawrence Summers is heading a U.S. delegation that will meet on Saturday with Japanese officials to discuss how to deal with Japan's woes. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Bank of New York President William McDonough, a former private bank executive and an expert on international banking issues, already is on his way to Tokyo. Fed Governor Roger Ferguson, who deals with banking issues at the Fed Board, also is part of the delegation. \par \par \uc2  A foreign central banker told Reuters last week the United States and other western economic powers had told Japan that they would not attempt to support the yen unless Japan was prepared to deal with fundamental issues. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates reflect fundamentals and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  do not work for long unless the fundamentals change," the foreign central banker said. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are effective once markets are convinced about governments' resolve to deal with their problems." \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide to a near eight-year low of 146.75 to the U.S. dollar on Tuesday had prompted market fears that China's yuan - the only major East Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to have escaped the region's devaluation contagion - may falter along with the yen. \par \par \uc2  Sun Zhenyu, China's vice minister for foreign trade, told Reuters a yuan devaluation may be necessary if the tumbling yen hurts Chinese exports further. \par \par \uc2  "It was not the comments overnight that prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the fear of what China might be forced to do (with its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), the fear the Chinese would be put in an untenable position, was the trigger behind this," said chief economist Ian Shepherdson at HSBC Securities. \par \par \uc2  "China has been very helpful to the (stability of the region) by not devaluing so far. But the U.S. administration and the Chinese government were feeling it (yen's fall) was very hard to sustain," Shepherdson noted. "The fear was that if China devalues, everybody else in Asia would follow suit, which would trigger a new financial collapse and an even longer recession." \par \par \uc2  ((-N.A. Treasurys Desk, 212-859-1666). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02oyw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
