\par\par \b \uc2  Sense of urgency seen joint  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  trigger. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Al Yoon \par \uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 21:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 17 (Reuters) - The potential that a more dizzying yen slide might trigger another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations in Asia put an urgent spin on Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  A sense of immediacy brought on by a fear of impending economic crisis separated Wednesday's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's solo efforts in December and April, they added. \par \par \uc2  The yen's decline had been gaining momentum, rising almost seven yen in the past week, likely sending the United States and Japan scrambling to plug the gap before the yen's haemorrhage reversed any plausible chance of economic recovery in Southeast Asia and other global emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan and U.S. Federal Reserve coordinated an estimated $6 billion in dollar sales for yen early in New York on Wednesday, knocking dollar/yen sharply lower. \par \par \uc2  "The atmosphere in the last three weeks is very different than the atmosphere in April ... in particular the view that many policy officials have adopted that unless this is stopped the yen could go to 150, 160, 170 and up," said Selig Sechzer, international economist at Alliance Capital Management. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  marks the first time the Fed has sold dollars for yen since February 1992. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  tried to shore up the yen through solo \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April and December. \par \par \uc2  Economists said failure to stabilise the yen now would pave the way for a domino effect-style financial disaster that would affect all countries in the world - not just Asia. \par \par \uc2  Steeper falls in the yen would almost certainly trigger a chain of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations, led by the Chinese yuan, Hong Kong dollar and probably the Russian rouble, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The consequences of such a rupture are simply too large to contemplate," Sechzer said. "Unlike in April the expectation of further yen weakness has galvanised G7 officials to take a much more aggressive approach to the problem." \par \par \uc2  High-profile objections to the yen's fall by China accelerated those fears, said Richard Koss, international economist at MFR Group of Funds. \par \par \uc2  The reluctance of the United States to join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its December and April \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  was, in part, meant to add pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pull its economy out of a tailspin with fundamental reforms and fiscal measures. \par \par \uc2  But this time, some analysts have speculated that Japan has agreed to key policy changes urged by the G7 nations. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the recovery in the yen will have to be supported by "very significant" policy initiatives by Japan. In fact, some said U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin must have solid assurances from Japan in order to put the credibility of the U.S. Treasury on the line through dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  "With the passing of Japan's supplementary budget ... the key here is we will probably see some concrete announcements as to what will be done in terms of policy," said David Durrant, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at I.D.E.A., who added "now Japan has the right to spend the money. That's what's different." \par \par \uc2  Rubin on Wednesday said he felt Japanese officials had a fundamental understanding of the issues and recognised the need to address them "in a serious fashion." President Bill Clinton said Japanese Prime Minister promised aggressive measures to stimulate Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  MFR's Koss also said the U.S. was beginning to feel a tide of negative sentiment from domestic companies directed against Japan and the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  "There have been a chorus of comments from U.S. companies, in particular, that the yen was starting to get to be more than an irritant in the earnings outlooks at current levels and backed up by a chorus of investors with the same concerns," Koss said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock markets responded positively to the revived yen, with the Dow industrials up more than 200 points and the Nasdaq Composite rising 32 points in afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  ((-N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1672)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02onh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
