\par\par \b \uc2  Analysts Doubt Treasury Rally Has Ended With Stronger Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Guy Dixon and John Montgomery, Staff Reporters \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 22:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The rug was suddenly yanked out from under the Treasurys market Wednesday, after having benefited for weeks from a soaring dollar and a safe-haven bid. \par \par \uc2  But while the downdraft in Treasurys appeared severe this session, many strategists say it may be a passing phase rather than a reversal in the market's fortunes. \par \par \uc2  The abrupt turnaround came after joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Federal Reserve and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen sent Treasury prices reeling. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, which began just as New York financial markets were opening, sent the dollar plunging to around 136.64 yen by late afternoon Wednesday from 143.33 yen late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Treasurys bounced slightly from their early lows, and market watchers say this session's events may be a temporary plot twist, rather than a permanent turning point in the Asian drama and the safe-haven supporting role for U.S. Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  "A lot depends on how well this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sticks," said William Kan, a Treasury market analyst at Merrill Lynch in New York. The question remains whether one morning's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is enough to undo the mounting doubt about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's intentions to implement reforms. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the Treasury's 30-year long bond backed up to 5.73% from 5.65% Tuesday and an all-time low of 5.58% Monday. But although the market retracted, the long bond's yield remains below 5.75% - still seen as a key psychological barrier. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, despite pledges by Japan's Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga to spur domestic demand and take all steps necessary to rebuild the Japanese banking system, investors may continue to wait out any rounds of Japanese reforms by staying parked in U.S. Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  "A lot needs to be done over the next several days," Kan added. "I wouldn't be surprised if some (safe-haven buying) came back into Treasurys," he said, noting that "the crisis in Asia is still looming over the market." \par \par \uc2  Jack Malvey, Lehman Brothers' chief global fixed-income strategist, indicated that while the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps to treat symptoms of the Asian flu, "I wonder if it's really treating the underlying root cause." \par \par \uc2  As a result of this uncertainty, Malvey saw the market polarized between investors focused on the downside effects of the Japanese recession and those keying on U.S. domestic strength. \par \par \uc2  For investors more concerned with Japan's problems, Malvey said they "would probably look at this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a brief respite." \par \par \uc2  Yet, some analysts see a clear change in conditions for U.S. Treasurys. "The joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  dramatically alters the picture," argued Richard Gilhooly, fixed-income strategist at Paribas Corporation in New York. \par \par \uc2  Gilhooly emphasized not only Japan's more aggressive effort to stabilize the yen and implement reform, but also the cooperative \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the U.S. "There appears to have been some bargaining behind the scenes and now they appear to have reached some sort of agreement," he said. \par \par \uc2  But he added that as the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ripples through the markets, Treasurys will remain under pressure, as the rush of overseas investors into Treasurys is quelled. "The safe-haven bid is over," said Gilhooly point blank. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, many safe-haven investors in Treasurys are looking for lasting Japanese reforms such as a permanent income-tax cut, rather than "a smoothing-over \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Josh Stiles, fixed-income strategist at IDEA in New York. "If Japan continues to languish, then the (Treasurys) market will come right back," Stiles added. \par \par \uc2  With Treasurys now taking their cue from developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, investors will stay particularly attuned to hints of any change in U.S. dollar policy. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin typically hints at an aversion toward \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unless absolutely needed, said analysts. Ultimately, Treasury investors will remain focused on whether Japan indeed continues to need help. \par \par \uc2  -By Guy Dixon 201-938-4148 and John Montgomery \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  4231 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du6h00wqd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
