\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis At A Glance: Dlr Halts Upward Climb Vs Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 491 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1998\par 10:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- The dollar fell sharply against the yen after traders, already nervous about yen-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold dollars in a panic amid talk that overseas funds sold large amounts of dollars. At 0830 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y144.80, down from Y146.15 late Monday in New York. The dollar fell as low as Y142.80 intraday. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- The Singapore dollar, Malaysian ringgit, Philippine peso and Thai baht rose against the U.S. dollar on profit-taking, after the greenback's rise against the yen halted. But South Korea's stock index closed 2.8% lower. Thailand's key index was down 3.2%, Malaysia's was down 3% and Indonesia's was down 2.3%, on economic bearishness. \par \par \uc2  -- Asia's financial crisis has spread into commodity prices and other emerging markets, making the outlook for the yen more bearish, said Ed Yardeni, chief economist of Deutsche Bank Securities. \par \par \uc2  -- Malaysia's Finance Minister Anwar said the yen's weakness is the result of slow action in tackling Japan's economic problems, and Tokyo's "footdragging" is harming the rest of the region. [*R/MY] [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- The Bank of Japan painted a gloomier picture of the Japanese economy, saying "production continues to decline, and employment and income conditions have recently shown significant deterioration." [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Japan is leading Asia into a new financial crisis that will put renewed pressure on China's exports and economic growth targets, Chinese government economists warned. \par \par \uc2  China's state-run media launched a campaign to restore investor confidence, saying mainland markets remain insulated from the region and that investor concerns about a crash in stock prices here is unfounded. [*R/CH] \par \par \uc2  -- Japan's life insurance industry suffered its worst investment performance on record in the year ended March 31, hit by low domestic interest rates and a depressed Tokyo stock market, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will maintain its market-determined \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy despite the peso's latest losses, linked to the yen's recent weakness. [*R/RP] \par \par \uc2  -- Bank Indonesia will maintain high interest rates though they haven't supported the value of the rupiah, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Sjahril said. [*R/IO] \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong Chief Executive Tung said he sees the Asian financial crisis continuing for "a number of years" before recovery begins. He also reiterated his commitment to maintaining the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar and urged China to resist calls to devalue the yuan. [*R/HK] \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore's Trade Minister said he doesn't expect the economy to slip into recession this year. [*R/SN] \par \par \uc2  -- Bankers Trust New Zealand said New Zealand's economy is in recession, according to its expectations for GDP growth in the first and second quarters of 1998. [*R/NZ] \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 16-06-98 \par \par \uc2  0853GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6g054h5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
