\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen wins respite from bears. \b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1998\par 16:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 16 (Reuters) - The yen won a respite on Tuesday, clawing its way back from new troughs against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Expectations that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be mired in recession for some time helped drive the yen to 146.75 per dollar overnight, its weakest in nearly eight years. The weakness which yen losses has sparked in other Asian markets also bolstered the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The yen quickly rebounded as much as 2.9 percent from these lows, however, as fear of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spurred traders to take profits on some of their long dollar positions. Still, this does not mark the end of yen weakness, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "We have had a big clear out overnight with the initial selling (in dollar/yen) triggered because everybody was nervous about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Juergen Lindemann, senior trader at IBJ in London. \par \par \uc2  "But while the downmove in dollar/yen may not be over yet, everybody still thinks the yen will stay on the soft side in the medium term and that we have not yet got a big turnaround." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 144.01/06 yen at 1422 GMT from 146.22/32 late in Europe on Monday. Mark/yen was at 80.22/25, weaker than the five-and-a-half-year high of 80.92 it set overnight but up from 79.97/00 yesterday. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, dollar/mark was largely sidelined, content to take its cue from dollar/yen, mark/yen and sterling. The dollar was at 1.7959/69 marks from 1.8128/38 late in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials kept up their efforts to support the yen on Tuesday. Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto said Japan was determined not to be the cause of a global depression while Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami detailed ways in which the bank's policy board had considered easing monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  These included steps such as lowering the reserve requirement of private banks and adjusting guidelines for overnight call rates, Hayami said. \par \par \uc2  However, such comments did not manage to deflate bearishness about the yen's prospects, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The obstacles in front of dollar/yen are falling rather than rising," Avinash Persaud, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at JP Morgan, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "Solitary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... will definitely not work. Concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the U.S. behind it and a shift in monetary policy would work." \par \par \uc2  Concern about the impact of yen weakness on Asian economies has already taken its toll on the Australian and New Zealand dollars, which today hit 12-year lows against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Back in Europe, sterling rose against the dollar and hit its highest in over a month against the mark after a report showed British inflation accelerated at a faster-than-expected pace in May and once again overshooting the government's target. \par \par \uc2  Comments made on Tuesday by members of the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee also kept talk of higher British interest rates alive. In particular Bank of England Governor Eddie George said the UK economy was closer to overheating than it had been for a long time. \par \par \uc2  "This adds to the weight of argument that rates may have to move higher still," said Deutsche Bank economist James Barty. \par \par \uc2  "Sterling looks very well underpinned and may be heading a little bit higher." \par \par \uc2  The pound was at session highs of 2.9710/20 marks at 1430 GMT from 2.9637/42 at the close of European trading on Monday. It was at $1.6532/42 from $1.6339/49 yesterday. \par \par \uc2  There was little out of the European Union summit in Cardiff to jolt the mark against other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. EU leaders said at the summit Europe's economic outlook had improved despite problems in Asia. ((Swaha Pattanaik, London newsroom, +44 171 542 6284, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2       Treasuries prices, yields. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6g02veb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
