\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr/yen ends US down, edgy market eyes T-bond, BoJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1998\par 22:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 16 (Reuters) - Dollar/yen fell on Tuesday after a report that a top U.S. Treasury official would attend emergency meetings on Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and amid lingering effects of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rumours, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was also lower in sympathy with the dollar-yen fall, and as the German unit took solace in improved Russian financial markets, where stocks rose 3.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar dipped near midday after a Japanese press report said U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers scheduled an emergency meeting with top officials from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to discuss the yen's fall and shaky Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury Department confirmed Summers was considering a trip to Tokyo for talks on the economic situation but there was no final decision and no date had been set. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin also confirmed a trip was being considered, while at the same time reiterating the U.S.'s "great concern about the weakness of the yen." He added that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sometimes appropriate, and sometimes not. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen, already sharply lower after BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rumours overnight, ended off at 143.25/35 yen, down from 144.20/30 at the open and 146.08/18 on Monday. It was also lower at 1.7980/90 marks from 1.7994/97 at the open. \par \par \uc2  Traders said any hint that the U.S. might change its strong dollar policy would cause the market to tumble but a revision in that stance was seen unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "There's still been no change in U.S. government policy toward Japan ... the U.S. does not want to use exchange rates as a factor in setting policy, said John Gariano, a vice president at Sumitomo Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts have speculated G7 nations might see fit to stem the yen's slide to prevent further turmoil in emerging markets and competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations in China, Hong Kong and Southeast Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  Yen sentiment improved overnight as rumours of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  erased four days of dollar gains, reflecting an increasingly edgy market as the greenback hit its best level in nearly eight years. \par \par \uc2  "The fear of the Bank of Japan coming in is very much alive and the proof in the pudding was last night ... it became a bloodbath without their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all," said John Schein, a vice president at Generale Bank of Belgium. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slid from 146.75 yen, its highest level since August 1990, to a session low of 142.65 overnight after the BoJ rumours first circulated in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  However, traders stressed the desire to sell yen was intact as the market kept its focus on Japan's poor economic fundamentals and shaky financial sector, which have contributed to the yen's 25 percent drop over the past year. \par \par \uc2  China stepped up its objections over the yen's fall and its draining effect on the Chinese economy. Officials in China insisted the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, would remain stable amid the pressure, however. \par \par \uc2  China's foreign ministry spokesman Zhu Bangzao urged Japan and "relevant" countries to "face reality and take effective measures to stop the yen's slide." \par \par \uc2  Traders noted BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk sent U.S. Treasuries sharply lower, with the long bond ending off more than one point. Treasuries had benefited for days as the yen and Asian markets plummeted, pushing investors to relative safe-havens. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar ended lower at 1.4960/70 Swiss francs from 1.4990/00 at the open and at Canadian $1.4697/02 from C$1.4711/16. The British pound firmed to $1.6510/20 from $1.6485/95, buoyed as higher than expected U.K. inflation data opened up speculation of another interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar was stronger at $0.5938/43 from $0.5904/09 at the open. \par \par \uc2  ((-Al Yoon, N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1672)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6g02u72\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
