\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slides against yen in nervous market. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Al Yoon \par \uc2 514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1998\par 22:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 16 (Reuters) - The dollar fell sharply against the yen on Tuesday amid rumours the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and after a report a U.S. Treasury official would attend emergency meetings on Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It was also down against the mark in sympathy with the dollar-yen fall, and as the German unit took solace in improved Russian financial markets where stocks rose 3.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  Rumours from Singapore that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets erased four days of gains for the dollar against the yen, reflecting an increasingly edgy market as the greenback hit its loftiest levels in eight years. \par \par \uc2  "The fear of the Bank of Japan coming in is very much alive, and the proof in the pudding was last night ... it became a bloodbath without their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all," said John Schein, a vice president at Generale Bank of Belgium. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slid from 146.75 yen, its highest level since August 1990, to a session low of 142.65 after the Bank of Japan rumours hit in Tokyo trading time. The trend to sell yen resumed quickly as traders refocused on Japan's negative economy and financial sector instability, which has driven the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down 25 percent in the past year. \par \par \uc2  In late trading, the dollar was at 143.35 yen, down from 146.13 Monday. It was lower at 1.7990 marks from 1.8085. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's recovery was stunted near midday after a Japanese news report said U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers scheduled an emergency meeting with top officials from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to discuss the yen's rapid fall and shaky Asian financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury Department confirmed Summers was considering a trip to Tokyo for talks on the economic situation but no final decision had been made. Traders said any hint that the United States might change its strong dollar policy could roil markets, but that any revision was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "There's still been no change in U.S. government policy toward Japan.... The U.S. does not want to use exchange rates as a factor in setting policy, said John Gariano, a vice president at Sumitomo Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said Group of Seven nations might see fit to at least brake the yen's fall to ward off further turmoil in emerging markets and competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations in China, Hong Kong and Southeast Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  China stepped up its objections over the yen's fall and its draining effect on the Chinese economy. Officials in China insisted the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, would remain stable amid the pressure, however. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar was lower at 1.4967 Swiss francs from 1.5057 Monday and at Canadian $1.4700 from C$1.4742. Britain's pound firmed to $1.6515 from $1.6360, buoyed as higher-than-expected British inflation data opened up speculation of another British interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6g02u57\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
