\par\par \b \uc2  Treasury's Summers May Head To Tokyo For Emergency Economic Talks \b0\par\par\uc2 1,239 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1998\par 21:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON -(Dow Jones)- The Treasury Department might soon send Deputy Secretary Lawrence Summers to Tokyo for emergency discussions on the weak yen and moribund Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  Treasury confirmed Tuesday that the trip was under consideration, but said that a final decision hasn't been made. As for the agenda, it said any visit would focus on economic matters. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, repeating that the U.S. continues to be concerned that the soft yen could worsen the Asian financial crisis, is still determining whether to send Summers. \par \par \uc2  "It might be an appropriate time" for him to go, Rubin said. He was holding another meeting later Tuesday to discuss the possible trip. \par \par \uc2  But other administration officials said details of the likely trip are expected to be set by Wednesday, and said the visit could occur as soon as Thursday. The officials, speaking on condition of anonymity, confirmed that the Treasury and Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been discussing a visit by Summers within the coming days. \par \par \uc2  "It's (the yen) been of enormous concern to us," Rubin said. "We have been very actively involved in talking to Japan for the past 18 months, publicly and privately, about weakness in the economy, he said. U.S. officials continue to have deep concerns about the absence of strong actions by Japan to revamp its ailing banking sector, and other steps that would provide a boost to the troubled Asian financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The Nihon Keizai Shimbun newspaper Wednesday cited unidentified U.S. officials in reporting that Summers would indeed make the trip and would bring up with his Japanese counterparts the yen's recent fall, the shrinking Japanese economy and instability in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The White House, meanwhile, said it is considering several ideas on steps it might take in regard to the intractable Asian financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "We have a range of ideas that we are exploring," White House spokesman Mike McCurry said, but wouldn't elaborate. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the yen hit a near-eight-year low against the dollar - 146 yen to the dollar - amid comments from Japanese officials hinting that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the struggling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is imminent. \par \par \uc2  There is increasing doubt, however, that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could stem the yen's fall even if it does \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic fundamentals are simply too weak and other countries have shown little enthusiasm for propping up the yen. The yen's decline was only aggravated further by Tokyo's announcement last Friday that the economy contracted at a 5.3% annualized rate in the latest quarter, confirming Japan has entered a recession. \par \par \uc2  In New York \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealings Tuesday, the yen rebounded against the dollar, in part on word of Summers's possible trip and on dollar profit-taking. In late dealings, the dollar was quoted at 143.33 yen, down from 146.15 yen late Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Top U.S. officials have said that the U.S. has few options for dealing with the Japanese situation. Indeed, Rubin said repeatedly last week the responsibility for halting the yen's slide rests with the Tokyo government. He said the only only way to strengthen the yen is for Japan to bolster its economy and reform its troubled financial sector, where a pile of bad loans resulting from the specualtive bubble of the late 1980s has left banks afraid to make new loans. \par \par \uc2  McCurry also made it clear that the White House expects Japan to take further action to revive its economy. \par \par \uc2  "In commenting on the fiscal stimulus package the Japanese government has put forward, we have applauded some of the steps they have taken, but we have also suggested they could work in concert with other steps that would produce the kind of economic performance that would restore some stability to the Asian regional economy and lead to demand-led growth for imports in Japan," McCurry said. \par \par \uc2  The White House has been prodding Japan to boost the demand side of its economy so that it may help crippled Asian neighbors rebound by importing their goods. Meanwhile, a further decline in the yen, which some analysts see as possibly hitting as low as 160 to the dollar, could lead to a devastating round of competitive devaluations of other weak Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It also may be important for the U.S. to send a strong signal of displeasure with Japan's economic-policy problems ahead of a landmark summit in Beijing between President Clinton and Chinese leader Jiang Zemin, some officials acknowledge. \par \par \uc2  There are growing concerns in financial markets, and within the Clinton administration, that a knee-jerk move by China to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could reignite a downward spiral throughout Asia's financial markets. Chinese officials have surprisingly stressed in public that the U.S. shares a role in pushing Japan to address its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  troubles. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile Tuesday, the World Bank's chief economist added his voice to the calls for Japan to get its growth back on track. Stopping short of making mention of any additional reform measures, Joseph Stiglitz simply reiterated that the Japanese economy must recover. \par \par \uc2  "Whatever needs to be done to restore their economy ought to be done," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government unveiled a 16 trillion yen economic stimulus measure this spring, but any benefits from the public-spending and tax-cut package won't manifest themselves until later this year. And the benefits of many of those pump-priming moves, such as the tax cuts, will only be temporary. \par \par \uc2  The European Union, wrapping up a summit in Cardiff, Wales, echoed Stiglitz's comments. EU finance ministers called for long-term measures to reform the Japanese economy, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to boost the yen wasn't discussed, according to a United Kingdom spokesman. \par \par \uc2  For its part, Treasury won't say whether a decision has been made on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although Rubin has underlined that fundamentals guide exchange rates and that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation is something that has a very short half-life, he also said last week that he wouldn't hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Sales of dollars by the U.S. is something the Clinton administration has never done. But Rubin has been laying a rhetorical foundation for months in which he has shifted attention to the weakness of the yen, rather than an over-valuation of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Administration officials haven't quibbled with the notion that Rubin has made a case for the dollar's march higher being a reflection of weakness in the yen, rather than a manifestation of an inappropriately strong dollar. However, the problem for many months for Rubin and his aides has been that economic fundamentals have suggested a further weakening of the yen. \par \par \uc2  Though Japan clearly isn't out of the economic woods, there is potential for some shifts in economic fundamentals between Japan and the U.S. in the coming months. Japan's big stimulus package, once fully adopted, should boost growth in the second half of the year, according to International Monetary Fund officials. \par \par \uc2  And U.S. growth could slow as the impact of the Asia crisis hits, which in turn would take the heat off the Federal Reserve for an interest rate hike and provide some underpinning to the yen. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  4228 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du6g00w8z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
