\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Stocks Fall 1%, End Below 15000 On Growing Investor Pessimism \b0\par\par\uc2 1,036 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1998\par 08:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Tokyo stocks closed more than 1% lower Monday amid growing investor pessimism about the weak Japanese economy, with the key Nikkei average ending below 15000 for the first time since Jan. 13. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was strong in late Asian trading after earlier soaring above 145 yen for the first time in eight years. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, the blue-chip Nikkei 225 index fell 197.16 points, or 1.3%, to close at 14825.17, its weakest close since Jan. 13, when it ended at 14755.94. Losers outnumbered winners 7 to 4 as 312 million shares changed hands. Friday, the Nikkei average fell below 15000 for the first time since Jan. 14 on an intraday basis, but later rebounded to close 8.29 points higher, above 15000. \par \par \uc2  Japanese stocks fell amid worries the weakening yen may throw the rest of Asia into renewed economic turmoil. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, gross domestic product figures released Friday confirmed what many investors had feared: Japan's economy is in the midst of its deepest recession in over 50 years. The world's second-largest economy recorded its worst year in 1997 since the end of World War II, contracting 0.7%, the Economic Planning Agency said. \par \par \uc2  That sent investors scurrying for cover, dumping yen for dollars. One concern is that Japan may be forced to cut interest rates from already historic lows. An interest-rate cut would make overseas investments look more attractive because of their relatively higher rates of return. While the Bank of Japan's policy board decided Friday to leave rates unchanged, traders pointed out the vote wasn't unanimous, with some favoring a cut. \par \par \uc2  Poor performance of other Asian financial markets Monday also accelerated selling of Japanese stocks, traders said. Philippine shares closed 4.5% lower and South Korean stocks plunged 4.8%. Hong Kong stocks were almost 5% lower at midday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar was quoted at 145.45 yen, up from 144.33 yen late Friday in New York. It was also quoted at 1.8092 marks, up from 1.8061 marks in New York. Earlier in Asia, it soared to 145.58 yen, the dollar's highest level since Aug. 24, 1990, when it rose to 146.75 yen. \par \par \uc2  There is also increasing doubt that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could stem the yen's fall even if it follows through on threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic fundamentals are simply too weak, and other countries have shown little enthusiasm for propping up the yen. \par \par \uc2  A weak yen helps Japan's exporters at the expense of the rest of Asia. As the yen falls, Japanese products become cheaper in key markets like the U.S., snatching market share from Asian competitors. That deprives hard-pressed economies like Thailand of desperately needed export revenues and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, public and pension funds were seen supporting the index's downside with a new portion of fund allocation flowing into stocks. But selling pressure was much stronger amid the gloomy economic outlook, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Due to the continued steep fall of the yen and sluggish Asian financial markets, it's uncertain at which levels Tokyo stocks can be supported," said Sachio Ishikawa, general manager at Chuo Securities Co. \par \par \uc2  Life insurers and other institutional investors have placed heavy put options for Nikkei futures at 14000 to 14500 on the assumption that the key index might drop to that level, Ishikawa noted. \par \par \uc2  Amid such pessimism, the Nikkei average is likely to test this year's intraday low of 14546.25 set on Jan. 13, and if it breaks below that level, the key index could then fall to around 14300 - the lowest since 1995, traders said. \par \par \uc2  High-technology blue chips were mostly weaker Monday on profit-taking after gains last week on the weaker yen. The yen's fall makes the products of such exporters more price competitive overseas. \par \par \uc2  Sony fell 190 yen to 11,350 yen, Advantest lost 400 yen to 7,120 yen and Kyocera dropped 180 yen to 6,110 yen. All five major chipmakers were also lower. \par \par \uc2  But other international blue chips managed to post moderate gains on public and pension-fund buying. Toyota, Fuji Photo, Bridgestone and precision machinery makers, such as Minolta, Nikon, Olympus and Canon were higher. \par \par \uc2  Shares in major Japanese bank shares were weighed down on fears that the weakness of the Japanese and other Asian economies may boost problem loans of the banks, traders said. The Nikkei bank index fell 0.8%. \par \par \uc2  Among major banks, Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi fell 28 yen to 1,252 yen, Industrial Bank of Japan lost 25 yen to 760 yen, Sumitomo Bank retreated 37 yen to 1,165 yen and Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank lost 18 yen to 738 yen. \par \par \uc2  The securities-brokerage sector was the top decliner, falling 4.3%, followed by real-estate developers, oil refiners and the warehouse sector. \par \par \uc2  Among brokerage firms, Nomura Securities' stock fell 51 yen, or 3.6%, to 1,380 yen, its lowest price for this year. Shares in other major securities companies such as Nikko, Daiwa and Kokusai were also sharply lower on profit-taking following gains in recent weeks on the Nikko and Travelers Group Inc. alliance. \par \par \uc2  Shares in construction companies were mostly weaker after Moody's Investors Service said it will downgrade, or review for downgrading, credit ratings for some companies in the sector. Credit ratings of Fujita and Hazama were downgraded; those of Taisei and Obayashi were put on review for a possible cut; and Kumagai Gumi's rating outlook was changed to negative from stable. The ratings actions reflect Moody's concerns about the highly leveraged profiles of some of the companies, the sustainability of explicit and implicit supports from their main banks, and the tough operating environment in the construction sector amid the changing financial system in Japan, the rating agency said. The construction sector fell 0.9% Monday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, stocks in Nippon Telegraph & Telephone ended Monday at this year's low of 1.02 million yen. Traders attributed the fall to foreign-investor selling. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du6f00xp2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
