\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Persistent/Debate/Cure -2: Stronger Yen For Asia \b0\par\par\uc2 523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1998\par 01:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  But the point of the concern is this: Early this year, the rest of Asia had come to count on a still bigger advantage for its exports over Japanese exports than it now has. A few months ago, Asian governments and companies expected that their devalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would help lead to an early recovery and Western fund managers were elbowing each into what appeared to be rapidly recovering Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  Clearly a stronger yen is better for the rest of Asia. For every 1% the yen falls against the dollar, economic growth rates in the rest of Asia shrink about 0.1%, estimates C.H. Kwan, senior economist at Nomura Research Institute in Tokyo. By that reckoning, the yen's 21% fall against the dollar in the past year has wiped two percentage points off Asian growth rates. \par \par \uc2  As a result, stock buyers "have gone on strike," says Deep Kapur, regional strategist at Salomon Smith Barney in Singapore. "Generally the equity market is selling off on the thinking that economic recovery (for Asia) is a story for 2000, not for next year," he says. "I would blame much of that on the yen." \par \par \uc2  Members of Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto's cabinet have been vowing for weeks now to act "decisively" on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japanese Minister of Finance Hikaru Matsunaga once again declared last week that Japan will take "decisive action" to stop the yen's slide. \par \par \uc2  What could \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  do? \par \par \uc2  - \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , could buy yen and sell dollars in what traders call an "open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But such moves often aren't effective for more than a short period, especially when one \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  works alone. The Bank of Japan spent an estimated $20 billion in April trying to defend the yen, points out Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank, "but it had little lasting effect." \par \par \uc2  - Engineer joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Tokyo has clearly been arguing for what traders, at least, say is the most promising short-term option: help from U.S. and other Group of Seven nations in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Concurring with Tokyo are Asian leaders like Thailand's Supachai, who called last week for the G-7 to implement "another Plaza Accord" to prevent the yen from falling further. \par \par \uc2  But Washington has made clear that it looks dimly upon \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some in Asia suspect this is because a concerted weakening of the dollar might cause foreigners to pull money out of the U.S. and thus disrupt U.S. markets. "Washington," says Ryuichi Takami, a senior trader at Sanwa Bank Ltd., "is not going to be overly anxious to help." \par \par \uc2  - Raise interest rates. A big reason the yen is weak is that Japanese interest rates are much lower than anywhere else - the lowest in world history by some measures. Short-term rates are 0.5%. Deregulation has just made it easier for Japanese to invest overseas, spurring investors to buy higher-interest dollar investments such as U.S. Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 14-06-98 \par \par \uc2  2323GMT \par \par \uc2  1122 4276 62542 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj