\par\par \b \uc2  Prospects/Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -4: Risks In Waiting Too Long \b0\par\par\uc2 349 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 1998\par 14:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Some market professionals argue that despite the official complaints about the yen's weakness, Japan is secretly quite pleased about the situation as it is keeping the country's exporters afloat. \par \par \uc2  They consider that, because of the weakness of domestic demand in Japan, a depreciated yen is the best thing going for the economy at present, as it makes Japanese goods and services more attractive abroad. \par \par \uc2  But other economists note a weaker yen only benefits Japan's manufacturing sector, which is in relatively good shape compared to the services sector, whose notoriously low productivity is a major brake on the economy. \par \par \uc2  Analysts who contend a depreciated yen is in Japan's overall economic interest fail to see the real problem of the Japanese economy is its financial sector, according to one Paris-based international economist. \par \par \uc2  The yen's weakness is a symptom of waning confidence of market operators in the Japanese government's political resolve to tackle the structural shortcomings of the banking industry, he said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the yen while these urgent reforms aren't implemented "would be like doing cosmetic surgery on someone who has cancer." \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, there are risks to the current policy of non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  As the dollar heads toward Y150.00, it is sparking further declines in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets in the region, and is starting to attract criticism from China. Many fear that if China is forced into a devaluation, it could spark a fresh crisis in the region that could prove even more disastrous than it has been so far. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One theory now gaining credence in Tokyo is that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't seriously\par engage in dollar-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  until the dollar rises to Y150,\par the rational being that the U.S. considers such a level would definitely\par damage other Asian economies.\par   -By David Pearson, with contributions from Dow Jones Newswires\par bureaus in Frankfurt, London, New York and Tokyo.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (33-1) 53-00-03-03; dpearson@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 12-06-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1256GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1193 4208 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6c051gh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
