\par\par \b \uc2  Weak yen struggles in Europe, Japan econ woes bite. \b0\par\par\uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 1998\par 12:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 12 (Reuters) - The yen continued to struggle against the dollar at the European mid-session on Friday as Japan's economic picture grew dimmer and prospects for concerted global \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen faded, economists said. \par \par \uc2  They said it was now only a matter of time before the dollar broke through 145 yen to pave the way for a decline to around 160 yen by year-end if Japan's economy remains on the sick bed. \par \par \uc2  "I think the yen is just in meltdown mode as a result of the dire economic fundamentals in Japan and the fact that Japan has officially gone into a recession," David Brown, chief economist at Bear Stearns in London said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's troubles deepened on Friday after Japan reported first quarter GDP fell a real 1.3 percent from the previous quarter, or an annualised drop of 5.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  Combined with a contraction in the fourth quarter of 1997, the data showed Japan has met the standard definition of recession. \par \par \uc2  Friday's announcement gave further weight to fears the world's second largest economy may not be able to engineer a recovery despite a 16 trillion yen ($111 billion) economic stimulus package announced in April. \par \par \uc2  Japan's woes have pushed the dollar up by a further 3.4 percent this week to a near eight-year high of 144.72 yen overnight, a far cry from its post-war low of around 80 in April 1995. \par \par \uc2  The yen's fate had also been worsened by U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin's comments on Thursday that only Japan could save the yen by igniting growth in its economy. \par \par \uc2  Yen bears saw Rubin's comments as meaning the United States was unlikely to help \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  through \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The yen is just going to continue sliding. The U.S. seems to be willing to put up with a weaker yen and Rubin has given it his blessing," said Bronwyn Curtis, chief international economist at Nomura International. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Russia remained a focus of attention after the International Monetary Fund (IMF) said it was in exploratory talks with Russia about raising extra cash to help its ailing financial markets. \par \par \uc2  Russia hopes the announcement will lead the IMF to release a $670 million disbursement from Moscow's existing $9 billion loan program. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar, which has been mired for much of the week around 12-year-lows against the U.S. dollar, trimmed its losses on Friday as buyers returned to the market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers cited heavy buying of Australian dollar/yen by a U.S. investment house, and talk of possible offshore bids for Australian assets, such as banks and resource companies, now that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's steep fall had made them cheaper. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar was last trading at US$0.5908/13 from a session low of US$0.5829 and US$0.5873/78 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The South African rand weakened sharply after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its repo rate by half a point to 17 percent. It hit a record low of 5.2920 to the dollar but rebounded slightly to 5.2840/70 at European mid-session. \par \par \uc2  Sterling benefited from the weak yen, rising to its highest level since September 1992 above 235 yen amid flows out of Asia into the pound, traders said. \par \par \uc2  ((Ellis Mnyandu, London Newsroom +44 171 542 6284, fax +44 171 583 3769, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (