\par\par \b \uc2  G-7 Central Banks Keeping  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Sword Sheathed For Now \b0\par\par\uc2  By David G. Pearson, Staff Reporter \par \uc2 854 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 1998\par 15:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  PARIS -(Dow Jones)- Concerted efforts to buoy the yen aren't likely as long as Japan's efforts to reform its own economic house aren't showing results. That was the message of U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin Thursday, and it is consistent with the views of other leading industrialized nations. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, there are some analysts who contend that despite its public protests to the contrary, Tokyo might be secretly pleased, at least in part, with the yen's depreciation since it helps keep exporters afloat. \par \par \uc2  That said, market participants would be wrong to think Group of Seven nations won't wield the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sword, analysts say, particularly if the yen's decline becomes truly disordered and heightens pressure on already troubled Asian economies. \par \par \uc2  In testimony before a U.S. Senate committee Thursday, Rubin signaled that the Clinton Administration feels concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's weakness wouldn't be effective while Japan's economic fundamentals are out of whack. \par \par \uc2  Frenzied yen-selling in North America Thursday pushed the dollar to 144.66 yen, its highest level since Aug. 30, 1990, although profit-taking brought it back in European and U.S. trading Friday. Around 9 a.m. EDT, the dollar was changing hands at 144.27 yen, up from 143.98 yen late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Rubin reiterated his view that while the U.S. shares concerns over the weak yen, the Japanese economy is failing to show signs of recovery. "The fundamental key to the yen ... is what happens inside \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "can be useful at times, can have an effect at times, but fundamentally over any period of time \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will follow fundamentals," Rubin said. He added the weakness of the yen "reflects economic conditions in Japan and can only be remedied by restoring economic strength in Japan." \par \par \uc2  The remarks were interpreted by market participants as a clear signal to Japanese authorities that, for now, they are on their own, although concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become an option when conditions in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  visibly start improving. \par \par \uc2  Rubin's remarks, they say, were an unequivocal message the U.S. and its allies feel \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's slide would be like pouring water on sand while Japan's economy shows no sign of bottoming out. \par \par \uc2  And that bottoming out seems unlikely in the near term. Friday, January-March gross domestic product data showed Japan has had two consecutive quarters of economic contraction, the textbook definition of a recession. GDP fell 1.3% in the first quarter of 1998 from the fourth quarter of 1997. GDP dropped 0.4% in the October-December quarter. \par \par \uc2  But despite that, U.S. monetary authorities aren't terribly worried about the yen's weakness, according to one London-based commentator, because it is keeping up pressure on Japan to make further efforts to deregulate its economy. In the meantime, he said, the strong dollar is helping the U.S. curb inflationary pressures, allowing the Federal Reserve to delay tightening monetary policy. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts say the likelihood of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase over coming months, but for now the safety catch is still on. \par \par \uc2  French Finance Minister Dominique Strauss-Kahn touched on the same theme as Rubin at a news conference Thursday, saying, "There's no decision for concerted action on the yen at the present time." \par \par \uc2  For its part, Germany traditionally disapproves of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially if it isn't backed by appropriate changes in policy and is aimed, as Japan would like, in the same direction the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is moving. \par \par \uc2  So it is unlikely to join a coordinated exercise unless the yen's fall turns into a crisis and so-called "disorderly markets" result. \par \par \uc2  "There are three cardinal rules for effective \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a senior official at a European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , only half jokingly. "They are: good timing; economic fundamentals that are in line with what you want to do; and good timing." \par \par \uc2  Failure to respect at least two of these rules will result in lost credibility, he said, as was witnessed in April when the Bank of Japan plunked down $20 billion to defend the yen, but was singularly unsuccessful. \par \par \uc2  But despite an international tide that is flowing against him, Japanese Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga reaffirmed Friday that his nation is ready to take decisive steps to prevent excessive weakening of the yen. He added, somewhat surprisingly in light of Rubin's remarks, that Japan's G-7 partners are ready to cooperate on such action. Along with the U.S., Japan, France and Germany, the United Kingdom, Italy and Canada make up the G-7. \par \par \uc2  Analysts and traders in Tokyo, however, generally believe Matsunaga is bluffing. According to one, "market sentiment right now is very, very dollar-bullish, so dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now would only provide good opportunities for people to accumulate dollar-long positions." \par \par \uc2  -By David Pearson (33-1) 53-00-03-03; dpearson@ap.org \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  1193 4208 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du6c00x4h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
