\par\par \b \uc2  Capital outflow hurting yen, little relief seen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 1,070 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 1998\par 11:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 11 (Reuters) - Capital flight from Japan is a significant factor in the yen's relentless slide and analysts say there are few signs the haemorrhaging will be staunched soon. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week said net purchases of foreign bonds by Japanese investors totalled 587 billion yen ($4.1 billion) in May after net purchases of a massive 2.7044 trillion yen in April. \par \par \uc2  Japan was also a net buyer of foreign stocks. \par \par \uc2  With yields so low in Japan and high and looking to go higher abroad, particularly in the United States and Europe, it was small wonder Japanese investors were looking overseas for better returns, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  There has even been talk that after the apparent failure of fiscal stimulus packages to boost the economy, Japan might be forced to cut interest rates, even though the Official Discount Rate (ODR) has been at just 0.5 percent for nearly three years. \par \par \uc2  "Given where the data and the fundamentals are going the likelihood is that capital outflows will increase and continue at a fairly high pace, so there will be a weaker yen," Richard Hostettier, Treasury Economist at West LB in Tokyo, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  The dollar chalked up a fresh seven-year high against the yen on Thursday, climbing to 142.10 yen in late afternoon trade in Tokyo, its highest level since June 1991. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that in the face of capital flows there was little central banks could do to in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and this could lie behind the lack of recent visible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It is probably also one of the reasons that others in G7 are reluctant to get involved with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a co-ordinated front, as they know they would get squashed by the steamroller of capital flows. \par \par \uc2  "There is money leaving Japan there's no doubt and that's one of the yen's problems," said Gerard Lyons, chief economist at DKB International in London. \par \par \uc2  "One argument is that Japan should raise interest rates to encourage more money to remain onshore...there has been a suggestion that there has been a lot of borrowing in yen for overseas markets, it's difficult to quantify but that's the implication." \par \par \uc2  Analysts also noted that Japanese households would also be receiving their half-yearly bonus in June and a lot of that was probably bound for investment in foreign assets. \par \par \uc2  In terms of capital flows, the boot is firmly on the other foot compared with the situation in the first half of the 1990s. \par \par \uc2  Japan's huge trade surplus then meant vast amounts of capital were flowing back onshore and tension between the United States and Japan became acute as the dollar sank to a record low of just under 80 yen in April 1995. \par \par \uc2  At the time the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market almost on a daily basis \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in an attempt to stop the yen from firming. \par \par \uc2  By the spring of 1995, Japan was on the brink of recession and financial meltdown as the very strong yen did major damage to exporters' balance sheets and government was forced to change policy. \par \par \uc2  This took the form of an agreement to weaken the yen, a cut in interest rates to just 0.5 percent and a huge fiscal boost of some 15 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  On top of this the government also lifted regulatory barriers on capital flows out of Japan, although at the time analysts doubted if this would unleash substantial outflow of investment. \par \par \uc2  But the moves were enough to quash the U.S./Japan trade row and in August 1995 the Federal Reserve joined the Bank of Japan in buying dollar/yen aggressively in the market. \par \par \uc2  This had a big impact on market sentiment although dollar/yen had already turned the corner on the back of the economic moves by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Also the combined \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  coincided with a break above a five-year downtrend line and the central banks succeeded in fuelling a sustained rise in dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  The subsequent fall in the value of the yen and low interest rates worked and by 1996 Japan was the fastest growing G7 economy with consumer spending providing the bulk of the growth. \par \par \uc2  But just as things began to look better, Japan decided to tighten fiscal policy, in 1997, which analysts said was the wrong thing to do at the wrong time. \par \par \uc2  This hit the economy in April and was closely followed by the Asia crisis in July which has seen stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumble. \par \par \uc2  Japanese consumers were hit hard by the tax increases and exporters and the corporate sector suffered from the Asia crisis. As the economy sagged a weakening yen became the only safety-valve. \par \par \uc2  But unless private sector confidence can be restored and at least some of the capital flight is stemmed there is little hope of a yen recovery, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  As in 1995, a shift in the U.S. position is needed to rescue the yen but after recent comments from U.S. officials there seems little hope of that. \par \par \uc2  While appearing to make the right noises, Washington has fallen short of offering Japan anything but advice. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin this week said that the United States shared Japan's concern about the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Our view is the same as it has been for many months, we shared and do share Japan's concern about the weakness of the yen," Rubin said. \par \par \uc2  But he added: "It is extremely important that Japan begin again to get back to a track of solid domestic demand-led growth, and in that respect Japan needs to deal with fiscal policy which as you know they have already done or are at least in the process of doing." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said this kind of comment pointed to a fairly relaxed U.S. attitude and showed it wanted to see Japan itself do a lot more about the weak yen. \par \par \uc2  "For domestic reasons it suits the Americans to have a strong dollar and for domestic reasons it did suit the Japanese to have a weaker yen," said DKB's Lyons. \par \par \uc2  "But we've reached the stage where it now starts to become potentially destabilising...if the yen weakens from here it doesn't have any obvious benefits to the Japanese economy." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6b0366l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
