\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar hits seven-yr high vs yen by Tokyo midday. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 1998\par 06:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      Bank of Japan corrects dollar high as of 0300 GMT to \par \par \uc2   141.88) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab     0300 GMT\tab      TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0300 GMT\par   USD/JPY     141.75/78\tab \tab   141.88-140.95\par   USD/DEM     1.7967/70\tab \tab   1.7977-1.7954\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO, June 11 (Reuters) - The dollar rose versus the yen by midday on Thursday, supported by the yen's weak prospects.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Falls in Japanese stocks and bonds were behind the dollar's rise to a seven-year high of 141.88 yen. But the dollar's momentum slowed as bulls were quick to take profits before testing the key 142 yen target, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nikkei stock average had fallen by two percent as of 0335 GMT, as bank shares were dealt a severe blow by renewed worries that Asia's troubled economies may hurt the sector.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said falls in bank shares hurt the Japanese government bond (JGB) market, which in turn, hurt the yen, which has been already been hampered by weaker economic fundamentals in Japan compared with the United States.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The yen has been sold due to falling JGB yields. But now that stocks and bonds were both sold today, that means Japan selling, or yen selling," said a dealer at a long-term credit bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some dealers said further Nikkei losses could trigger active yen sales.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Yen selling may accelerate if the Nikkei falls below 15,000," said a European bank dealer. The Nikkei was at 15,031.42 at 0342 GMT, down 307.84 points or 2.01 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A market focus now is the Liberal Democratic Party policy chief Taku Yamasaki's speech from 0300 GMT, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Japan's unresolved bad loan problem is one of the key negative factors for the yen. If Yamasaki makes bold remarks on this issue, yen buying may be triggered," said a dealer at a Japanese trust bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If not, that means dollar buying," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In early trade, the dollar's first upside test stopped at 141.75 yen due to offers by Japanese exporters including car makers and selling by U.S. fund operators.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar then slipped to a low of 140.95 yen after remarks by senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda, but their impact was short-lived, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The only way to block the uptrend is strong joint action. Verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  no longer seems to be effective after Deputy U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers failed to knock it (dollar) down yesterday," said a city bank dealer.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Kuroda told reporters that the Group of Seven finance ministers publicly agreed that an excessively weak yen is unfavourable and "they would take appropriate measures as needed. As for our country, we will take decisive measures".\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Kuroda also said he thinks China will keep its repeated pledges not to devalue its yuan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Asked about expressions of concern, especially from China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , about the effect of the yen's weakness on other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Kuroda reiterated that "excessive yen weakness, excessive depreciation of the yen, is unfavourable".\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Governor of the People's Bank of China, Dai Xianglong, on Tuesday urged Japan to act to bolster the yen, omitting the usual vow not to devalue the yuan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There is a view that both the United States and Japan may benefit from a strong dollar for the time being as it may tame U.S. inflation and help support \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's export-led growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But caution over possible joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept the dollar from rising further, dealers said. "There have been calls to correct the yen's weakness, most notably from the governor of China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . So we cannot rule out concerted international action on the yen," said a dealer.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nikkei stock average had fallen by two percent as of 0335 GMT, as bank shares were dealt a severe blow by renewed worries that Asia's troubled economies may hurt the sector.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said falls in bank shares hurt the Japanese government bond (JGB) market, which in turn, hurt the yen, which has been already been hampered by weaker economic fundamentals in Japan compared with the United States.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The yen has been sold due to falling JGB yields. But now that stocks and bonds were both sold today, that means Japan selling, or yen selling," said a dealer at a long-term credit bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some dealers said further Nikkei losses could trigger active yen sales.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Yen selling may accelerate if the Nikkei falls below 15,000," said a European bank dealer. The Nikkei was at 15,031.42 at 0342 GMT, down 307.84 points or 2.01 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A market focus now is the Liberal Democratic Party policy chief Taku Yamasaki's speech from 0300 GMT, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Japan's unresolved bad loan problem is one of the key negative factors for the yen. If Yamasaki makes bold remarks on this issue, yen buying may be triggered," said a dealer at a Japanese trust bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If not, that means dollar buying," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In early trade, the dollar's first upside test stopped at 141.75 yen due to offers by Japanese exporters including car makers and selling by U.S. fund operators.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar then slipped to a low of 140.95 yen after remarks by senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda, but their impact was short-lived, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The only way to block the uptrend is strong joint action. Verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  no longer seems to be effective after Deputy U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers failed to knock it (dollar) down yesterday," said a city bank dealer.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Kuroda told reporters that the Group of Seven finance ministers publicly agreed that an excessively weak yen is unfavourable and "they would take appropriate measures as needed. As for our country, we will take decisive measures".\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Kuroda also said he thinks China will keep its repeated pledges not to devalue its yuan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Asked about expressions of concern, especially from China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , about the effect of the yen's weakness on other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Kuroda reiterated that "excessive yen weakness, excessive depreciation of the yen, is unfavourable".\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Governor of the People's Bank of China, Dai Xianglong, on Tuesday urged Japan to act to bolster the yen, omitting the usual vow not to deval