\par\par \b \uc2  Japan, Asia locked in vicious circle of pessimism. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 921 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 1998\par 09:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Reuters) - Japan and its Asian neighbours appear locked in a vicious circle of pessimism that some fear risks pushing the region off the edge of an economic precipice. \par \par \uc2  But while many critics say it's up to Japan to forge policies to stave off a second round of regional crisis, the outlook for bold solutions from Tokyo is cloudy at best. \par \par \uc2  "We are near the precipice. I think we still got some time ... but things don't look good," said Ron Bevacqua, economist at Merrill Lynch Japan. \par \par \uc2  " ... Not only the corporate managers, who should have their ears to the ground (in Asia), but also the authorities are underestimating the problem and seem a bit complacent," he said. \par \par \uc2  Asian stock markets reeled Wednesday as concerns spread that a weakening yen would drag regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet lower and push China to devalue its yuan. Regional gloom boomeranged back to Japan, pushing the yen and stock prices yet lower. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Asian stock markets slid again. Tokyo stocks fell sharply as investors sold banks' stocks on worries that Asian woes would aggravate their huge bad loan headache. \par \par \uc2  Fears over Japanese banks' financial health also hit Japanese government bond prices, in turn causing the yen to give up earlier gains against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The greenback broke above 142 yen in Tokyo in afternoon trade, then dipped back to about 141.80. \par \par \uc2  Behind the latest bout of regional high anxiety is concern that the weak yen limits chances that Asian companies, especially direct Japanese competitors in South Korea and Taiwan, can export their way out of trouble. \par \par \uc2  The weak yen and the beleaquered Japanese economy that it reflects also mean chances are slim that Japan can suck in enough Asian imports to help the region recover. \par \par \uc2  Imports are more expensive when a country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is weaker. A slumping \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the yen, also makes expenses in that nation, such as salary or travel, cheaper for those with stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as U.S. companies or tourists. \par \par \uc2  Not everyone sees Japan as solely responsible for the latest crisis jitters. "The perception has become that the weak yen is bad for Asia, but, as far as I can see, the causality is the other way around - a weak Asia is causing people to buy dollars," said Richard Jerram, chief economist at ING Barings. "People have realised how bad the real economies in Asia are." \par \par \uc2  That said, asking other Asian countries to come up with solutions to pull the region back from the brink is probably fruitless, despite signs that countries like South Korea, Thailand and Malaysia are doing some of what needs to be done at home. \par \par \uc2  "Two-thirds of the region is Japan," said Jesper Koll, chief economist at J.P. Morgan & Co. Inc. in Tokyo. "Japan will always be the headwind." \par \par \uc2  Hopes that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the United States and other Group of Seven nations could break the vicious circle appear to have faded for now. \par \par \uc2  Washington seems reluctant to act, either because officials believe it would be futile given Japan's weak economy or because they are not persuaded a new crisis is at hand. \par \par \uc2  "Either there isn't a consensus that the yen/dollar rate could be stabilised, or more likely, there isn't a consensus that it needs to be stablized," ING Barings' Jerram said. \par \par \uc2  Most, therefore, place the onus for averting a fresh regional crisis squarely on Tokyo's policy shoulders. "What's the way out? Getting government policies under control in Japan," said Robert Feldman, chief economist for Japan at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  Topping the list of what pundits now say is needed to restore confidence in Japan's economic future is an aggressive plan to resolve the bad loan problem plaguing Japanese banks. \par \par \uc2  "They've had the largest-ever fiscal package, the lowest-ever interest rates, and the biggest-ever bank bailout package, and still they can't restore confidence," Koll said. "There is one way, and one way only, to fix the problem - one-third of the banks have to die, and die quickly. They have to close the banks and sell the assets to the highest bidder." \par \par \uc2  Authorities, however, are still seeking a "soft landing" for the banking system, and some ruling politicians say serious discussion of how to handle failed banks will not come until after a July 12 election for parliament's Upper House. \par \par \uc2  Japanese critics, meanwhile, are also urging the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to further ease monetary policy by cutting already rock-bottom interest rates and injecting more liquidity to avert a deflationary spiral. But not everyone agrees. \par \par \uc2  "We have to be careful whether measures are sufficient, or more than sufficient," former Vice International Finance Minister Kosuke Nakahira said in an interview this week, dubbing such reflationary steps "by far too much." \par \par \uc2  Optimists are thus in short supply. \par \par \uc2  "The yen must be the anchor ... and that means Japan must fix its economy," said Susumu Kato, chief economist at Barclays Capital Japan. "But there seems to be nothing they can do." \par \par \uc2  Some, however, say a good jolt of crisis jitters may be just what Tokyo needs. \par \par \uc2  "I think we're already in a second downwave now. Hopefully, this will serve as an impetus for more proactive policies in the region including Japan," said Larry Duke, vice president at Citibank in Tokyo. "This sort of gives warning that more needs to be done to stimulate domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6b035y3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
