\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Yen skids to fresh 7-yr low,euroshares wary. \b0\par\par\uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 1998\par 12:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 9 (Reuters) - The beleaguered yen sank to yet another seven-year low against the dollar on Tuesday, breaking the key 141 yen/dollar level before regaining some composure ahead of news from a Group of Seven (G7) meeting in Paris. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said there was nagging concern the G7 deputy finance ministers - who have said they will not discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - would agree on an action plan to try and turn the yen's fortunes around, even if there was no official announcement. \par \par \uc2  "The balance of risk is that something might still happen, and that has taken the steam out of the dollar for now," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC Wood Gundy Oppenheimer in London. \par \par \uc2  European share markets ignored a firm close on Wall Street overnight and an impressive 1.5 percent gain in Japan's Nikkei index to soften modestly at the session's start, as profit-takers reaped the rewards of Monday's record gains. \par \par \uc2  In London, the FTSE 100 index had dipped by around 30 points by 0940 GMT, but stuck stubbornly above the key 6,000 level, after breaking it for the first time in two weeks on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think there will be too much profit-taking," said one dealer. "People would rather pitch it a bit higher before we see sellers." The broader FTSE 250 index managed to stay in positive ground, up 4.5 points at 5,964.8. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were confident that losses would be limited and said that despite the thin volume seen in Monday's rally, the market was gathering momentum for its next push upwards. \par \par \uc2  The Dow ended 32 points better at 9,069.6, but gains in New York were tempered by caution ahead of Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan's comments on the economy to Congress on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  News of another huge bank merger in the U.S. had failed to spark a broad rally but hope was growing there that the recent consolidation in stocks may have run its course. \par \par \uc2  The German and French bourses also retreated cautiously, as concern over the dollar's weakening began to creep in. \par \par \uc2  By 0940 GMT, the yen was at 140.23 to the dollar - stronger than 140.47 in late Europe on Monday - after hitting 141.35 yen in early Asian trade. Against the mark, the yen was at 79.06 compared with 78.99 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the dollar was expected to resume its upward march once the G7 meeting was out of the way. \par \par \uc2  "The threat of co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to coincide with the G7 deputy finance ministers meeting is not seen as a credible one and has to be one reason why dollar/yen is expected to advance higher after tomorrow," said Michael Lewis, international economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar leaped to fresh highs against the yen after U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said overnight the G7 would discuss Russia along with the crisis in Asia, but were unlikely to make any policy announcements. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also boosted against the yen by rumours, later denied, that Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan was in financial difficulties. \par \par \uc2  Gains were curbed after Liberal Democratic Party official Taku Yamasaki said he had asked the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  for aggressive action on the yen, fuelling fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf, with or without G7 backup. \par \par \uc2  In Britain, investors were chewing over a heavy diary of corporate results including annual profits from Northern Foods, Severn Trent and Wessex Water. Shares in all three stocks were trading lower. \par \par \uc2  Among other features, oil stocks were softer on the back of losses in New York and a weakening oil price. BP fell 1.8 percent to 910-1/2 pence. \par \par \uc2  UK economic data released early in the morning barely caused a ripple. Manufacturing output edged up 0.1 percent in April but was 0.2 percent lower than a year earlier, while overall industrial production rose by 1.0 percent, more robust than expected. \par \par \uc2  Economists focused on the weak trend in manufacturing, with many still questioning last week's surprise decision by the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee to raise British interest rates a further 0.25 percent to 7.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6903gpa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
