\par\par \b \uc2  Feeble yen keeps up pressure on Asian markets. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stuart Grudgings \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 1998\par 08:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 9 (Reuters) - Japan's sliding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dampened Asian sentiment again in early Tuesday trade and Hong Kong markets were washed out by a rainstorm. \par \par \uc2  The yen fell below 141 to the dollar in early Asian trade and, despite a small rebound later, traders worried about the threat it poses to the competitiveness and stability of regional economies. \par \par \uc2  A perceived lack of concrete action by Japanese policymakers to stop the rot was another source of concern, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Shares in South Korea, which analysts say is most vulnerable to a weaker yen, took the worst hit, but Tokyo shares drew comfort from brighter export prospects to move higher. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong markets were closed in the morning after the territory was struck by torrential rains and residents were urged to stay at home. The local stock exchange said trade would begin in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly steady, but the Australian dollar remained weak despite growing talk of an interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  But the yen's fall remained the dominant issue throughout the region, whose recession-threatened economies are linked with Japan through trade and loans. \par \par \uc2  "There's a clear risk of further falls for Asian markets, it all depends on the pace of the yen's decline," said Shaun Roache, global strategist at ING Barings in London. \par \par \uc2  The yen had rebounded from a seven-year low of 141.35 to the dollar to 140.70 by early afternoon after official comments raised fears of possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga repeated his "strong worries over an excessive yen weakness", vowing that Japan would take "decisive measures as needed" in cooperation with the rest of the Group of Seven economies. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar remained well bid above the 140-level, with traders voicing scepticism over whether words would be backed by action. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks shrugged off gloom over the domestic economy to trade over one percent higher in the afternoon as companies set to benefit from a more competitive yen led the way. \par \par \uc2  The main Nikkei index was up 1.25 percent at 15,485.51. Among major exporters, Sony Corp was up 240 yen at 11,660 and TDK Corp was up 150 yen at 10,700. \par \par \uc2  "Large-lot buying came in exporters, such as Sony, which lifted the Nikkei average," said Kunihiro Hatae, general manager at Tokyo Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  But shares of Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan Ltd (LTCB) tumbled amid lingering worries over its financial health, dragging down some other bank shares, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Fears that the yen's slide would erode the competitiveness of South Korean exports sent Seoul shares diving by more than three percent early on, before an afternoon rebound helped improve sentiment. South Korean firms compete head-to-head with Japan on world markets in goods such as electronics. \par \par \uc2  "South Korean investors are fleeing the market because of fears that foreigners will pull out from Asian markets," said Choo Hee-yup at Dongwon Securities. "They are also concerned that a weak Japanese yen will hurt South Korean exports." \par \par \uc2  The composite stock index stood at 339.26 points, down 1.91 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar continued to languish below 60 U.S. cents, defying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and speculation that interest rates would be raised. \par \par \uc2  A rise in money market rates helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  poke its head above the 60-cent mark earlier, but it was soon beaten down to 12-year lows around 0.5965. \par \par \uc2  Other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were little changed as traders waited to see where the dollar/yen rate would head next. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was stagnant as traders refused to get excited about more encouraging sounds from the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  But the rupiah remained stuck around 11,500 to the dollar and Jakarta's main stock index edged down 0.63 percent to 406.83 in thin morning trade. \par \par \uc2  Southeast Asian markets were subdued. Malaysian shares found little to inspire them, closing the morning down 1.54 points at 496.25 and Singapore's main Straits Times Index was down 0.65 percent at 1,121.26. \par \par \uc2  Worries over Thailand's financial sector combined with yen-inspired nervousness to send Bangkok stocks down 1.37 percent to 308.09 by the midday break. \par \par \uc2  Mounting concern over the fading Taiwan dollar wiped out a modest stock market gain in Taipei, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The main index closed down 135.64 points, 1.79 percent, at 7,455.63 after the Taiwan dollar slid to an 11-year low of 34.751 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The key Philippine share index ended off 6.10 points at 1,924.49. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6903gbq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
