\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar/yen off highs in Europe as dealers await G7. \b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 1998\par 09:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 9 (Reuters) - The dollar settled about half a yen below overnight highs above 141 in cautious European trade on Tuesday as dealers awaited news from a meeting attended by Group of Seven deputy finance ministers. \par \par \uc2  G7 deputies will gather for a regular OECD working party review in Paris later on Tuesday. Waning speculation they would discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the sidelines of the meeting and come up with a plan to halt the yen's slide has boosted the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said there was some caution among traders now that even though no official policy announcements are expected, the G7 might agree an action plan without making it public. \par \par \uc2  "The balance of risk is that something might still happen, and that has taken the steam out of the dollar for now," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC Wood Gundy Oppenheimer in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to a fresh 7-year high just above 141 yen overnight after U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said the G7 would discuss the situation in Russia along with the crisis in Asia but were unlikely to make any policy announcements. \par \par \uc2  It was also boosted by rumours, later denied, that Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan was in financial difficulties. \par \par \uc2  Concern that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if there will be no concerted action by the G7 nations was fuelled after Liberal Democratic Party official Taku Yamasaki said he asked the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  for aggressive action on the yen. Dollar/yen fell to an overnight low of 140.20 after the comment. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the level is expected to act as support, ahead of the firmer previous resistance around 139.70/80. \par \par \uc2  At 0735 GMT the dollar was at 140.45/50 yen and 1.7763/66 marks, little changed from 140.47/57 and 1.7787/90 in late Europe on Monday. Mark/yen was a 79.06/09 from 78.99/79.02. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said dollar/yen is expected to resume its upward move once the G7 meeting is out of the way. \par \par \uc2  "The threat of co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to coincide with the G7 deputy finance ministers meeting is not seen as a credible one and has to be one reason why dollar/yen is expected to advance higher after tomorrow," said Michael Lewis, international economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in London. \par \par \uc2  A downbeat monthly report by the Economic Planning Agency (EPA) raised the spectre of more gloomy economic news on Friday, when Japan's first-quarter GDP figures are out. EPA said the severity of Japan's economic plight had increased as record-high unemployment dampened consumer sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The dollar shrugged off news that German unemployment fell by a seasonally adjusted 60,000 to 4.324 million in May, while the unemployment rate eased to 10.9 percent from 11.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  The figures were in line with expectations. Lewis at Deutsche said the decline - the largest in the current cycle - was due to government sponsored training programmes and not evidence of an acceleration in growth. \par \par \uc2  Even so, the more upbeat tone of recent German economic data is likely to bolster the mark, while possible news of G7 support for Russia out of Paris is also likely to underpin it. \par \par \uc2  The market will also be watching for developments from the first council meeting of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A news conference is scheduled for 1500 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The meeting is expected to focus on preparatory work and steer clear of policy issues such as European interest rate convergence. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar remained weak below $0.6000 but off its $0.5950 overnight low, trading at $0.5970/75 at 0815 GMT. \par \par \uc2  ((Astrid Zweynert, London newsroom, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 542 52 93, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du69039ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
