\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr/yen bid above 140 as market tests BOJ resolve. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Astrid Zweynert \par \uc2 772 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 1998\par 09:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 8 (Reuters) - The dollar remained bid above 140 yen on Monday as dealers braced themselves for further gains amid fading expectations that the Group of Seven nations (G7) would come to the aid of the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign so far of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the dollar's rush to a high of 140.73 yen - the highest since June 1991 - and weakening Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains were fuelled by a growing view that the G7 deputy finance ministers will not draw up with an agreement to halt the yen's slide when the meet in Paris on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Indications that the G7 deputies meeting tomorrow will focus on the situation in Russia rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the lack of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have seen the yen weaken," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economist at Bank America in London. \par \par \uc2  At 0745 GMT the dollar was at 140.48/53 yen, up from 139.64/74 in late Europe on Friday. Against the mark, the dollar was little changed at 1.7761/67 from 1.7744/54, while mark/yen cracked 79 yen to hit a peak of 79.20, its highest since December 1992. It last traded at 79.10/13, up from 78.71/74. \par \par \uc2  The latest yen slide was triggered by a comment from Koji Tanami, vice minister at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tanami said during Asian trading that the main topic of the deputies meeting would be Russia and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were unlikely to be a main topic. \par \par \uc2  The yen slid even though Tanami and other Japanese officials reiterated excessive yen weakness was undesirable and that Japan was ready to take decisive action. Tanami also said the U.S. and Japan had agreed to cooperate on excessive yen falls. \par \par \uc2  However, a later comment by Tanami, saying that Japan must consider the effect of the yen's fall on other Asian economies, knocked dollar/yen back to a European session low of 140.10. \par \par \uc2  But it is action not words that the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market wants to see, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The market remains unconvinced that concerted action will be taken," said a senior trader at a German bank in London. "But it's trading very nervously now that we're above 140 because the market is concerned that the BOJ might come in." \par \par \uc2  However, given \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dire economic fundamentals the value of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is questionable, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ is estimated to have spent about $20 billion on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April in an unsuccessful attempt to stem the yen's slide. It has dropped from around 129 per dollar in the past two months. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, the market is expecting the BOJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which makes the likelihood of successful \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action even less likely. \par \par \uc2  "The problem the authorities have is that the market is waiting for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that's when it's least likely to work," said the head of trading at a large U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  Despite a surge in Japan's trade surplus of 933.3 percent year-on-year the first 20 days of May, analysts said it was unlikely the U.S. would change its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Even though the firm dollar makes U.S. exports dearer, the world's largest economy is still going strong, as highlighted by a 296,000 rise in non-farm payrolls in May. \par \par \uc2  "As long as domestic demand is strong in the U.S. they can live with a firm dollar," said Tony Norfield, head of research at ABN AMRO in London. "It would take a blow-out in Asia to change the U.S. administration's mind." \par \par \uc2  U.S. economist Fred Bergsten said on Saturday the dollar could hit 150 yen in the short-term if central banks did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is based on the lack of confidence in Japanese economic policy," said Bergsten. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was likely to remain stuck though around current levels unless dollar/yen surged further, dealers said. The prospect of a strong German jobs figure this week has given the mark support. Chancellor Helmut Kohl said last week the jobless number would fall by about 200,000 to 4.2 million on an unadjusted basis. \par \par \uc2  Dealers will be watching out for comments from the annual meeting of the Bank for International settlements in Basle, which coincides with the regular monthly G10 central bankers meeting. ((London newsroom, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 542 52 93, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6803dm9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
