\par\par \b \uc2  Beleaguered yen plumbs new depths by Europe midday. \b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 1998\par 12:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 1 (Reuters) - Bears continued their assault on the yen in European trade as pessimism over Japan's economic fundamentals prevailed, though they were showing some restraint ahead of the key level of 140 yen to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Weak Japanese data and drops in Tokyo stock prices and government bond yields encouraged yen selling, while Japanese officials' failure to step up their rhetoric led the market to believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had further to fall. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities know \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be of little use unless it's accompanied by policy measures or (G7) cooperation," said Masahiro Kawasaki, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sakura Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Japan needs to have its exporters supporting the economy, and a weak yen will bring imported inflation to Japan which will help stave off any deflation. Which makes people think there's very little demerit in having a weak yen," Kawasaki added. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was quoted at 139.41/51 at 1050 GMT after hitting 139.68, its strongest in nearly seven years and up 0.6 percent from late Europe on Friday. The mark rallied to 78.50 yen, its highest since January 1993, compared to 77.88/92 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said dollar/yen may face a bit of a struggle at 140 as some participants were likely to sell dollars just below there to protect options with strikes at the round-figure. \par \par \uc2  Many agreed it was only a matter of time before economic fundamentals catapult dollar/yen above that key level. \par \par \uc2  "The 140 level is increasingly becoming a formality," said Claudio Piron, treasury economist at Standard Chartered adding that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if dollar/yen travelled too rapidly towards 150. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minister Koji Tanami said on Monday that Tokyo would take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as needed, adding that Japan and the United States were in agreement in their worry over excessive weakness in the yen. This was basically a reiteration of Japanese officials' recent remarks. \par \par \uc2  Analysts saw little respite for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near future. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously we're hopeful Japan's economic stimulus package will take hold but that will be a matter of months rather than the next couple of weeks," said Neil Parker, treasury economist at Royal Bank of Scotland. "So the yen will stay under pressure until we see some direct impact from the stimulus measures." \par \par \uc2  The mark, meanwhile, was getting some support from perceptions Russia's financial crisis had cooled off somewhat. Dollar/mark was quoted at 1.7762/72 against 1.7795/05 in late Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Late on Sunday, following talks between high-level U.S. and Russian officials, Washington said it would help mobilise more aid for Russia if needed from international lending agencies to help Moscow cope with the effects of Asia's economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  "They (Germany) are among those who have the biggest exposure to Russia so the mark would come under pressure if there were any problems. But the selling of marks that we saw last week over Russia was a bit overplayed anyway," said Parker. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said that regarding the mark, the focus will shift from Russia to European monetary union ahead of the first policy-setting meeting of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week. \par \par \uc2  The British pound held up against the dollar and mark as it shook off the impact of data showing weakness in the British manufacturing sector. The Chartered Institute of Purchasing and Supply said its purchasing managers' index of manufacturing activity fell to 49.3 in May from 49.5 in April. The export orders index was below the break-even 50 mark for the fifth straight month. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that although the numbers were weak, the depressed state of the manufacturing sector was already factored into the market. Sterling was quoted at 2.9020/30 marks and $1.6320/30 against 2.8996/06 and $1.6274/84 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom, +44 171-542-6721, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du61041hu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
