\par\par \b \uc2  AWSJ: Yen Tumble Against Dollar Not Seen Halting Soon \b0\par\par\uc2 346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 1998\par 01:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  From Thursday's Asian Wall Street Journal \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Bill Spindle\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--With the U.S. economy moving at full steam and Japan's losing it, the recent slide of the yen against the dollar isn't likely to stop any time soon, economists said. \par \par \uc2  That's bad news for the rest of Asia, which is taking the brunt of the pain a weaker yen is doling out. \par \par \uc2  The yen's rapid slide - to a seven-year low of 138 yen to the dollar on Tuesday from 131 yen at the beginning of the month - has come amid reports that the U.S. favors a stronger dollar and speculation that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  But economists said the move is really just a belated adjustment to economic fundamentals that have been in place for months - and that aren't likely to change in the next year. \par \par \uc2  The depreciation hadn't happened sooner, they said, because of concerns that the U.S. and Japanese governments might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent it, as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did in April. But those concerns diminished after financial leaders barely discussed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a recent summit and a U.S. magazine reported that U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin would tolerate a dollar that bought as much as 150 yen. Rubin later said he shares Japan's concern about a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  Even before that, however, several economists in Tokyo were saying the dollar will probably strengthen to about 150 yen during the next 12 months. \par \par \uc2  Both in Washington and on Wall Street, the yen's weakness is viewed as an almost inevitable result of financial markets' dismay with Japan's economy and its economic policies. Renewed recession in Japan has been matched with unrelenting growth in the U.S.; speculation about easier monetary policy in Tokyo has been matched with speculation about tighter monetary policy in the U.S. So the dollar is rising. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 27-05-98 \par \par \uc2  2307GMT \par \par \uc2  1126 4276 62546 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5r04jfp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
