\par\par \b \uc2  Mark/yen flirts with new highs in midday Europe. \b0\par\par\uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 1998\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 26 (Reuters) - The mark clamoured through key resistance levels to touch new five-year highs versus the yen in midsession Europe on Tuesday, and traders expect the cross to climb higher in coming days. \par \par \uc2  "Mark/yen was pushing through new highs this morning. I expect to see it higher, probably near 80.00 in the next couple of weeks," said James McKay, chief European economist at Commonwealth Bank of Australia. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said concerns about the weak Japanese economy and the contrasting picture emerging in Germany will continue to see funds channelled into Germany from Japan. \par \par \uc2  By 1045 GMT mark/yen was trading at 77.92/96 versus a session peak of 78.02 and 77.65/66 in late European trade on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said the market was using the mark as a proxy for the dollar versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Some people prefer to buy marks against the yen rather than dollars because \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the yen will normally be against the dollar," a trader said. \par \par \uc2  Although the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not, as was widely expected, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen on Monday, analysts said the market remained wary of an ambush. \par \par \uc2  "Sell the yen, but don't hold long dollar positions overnight," said Mark Geddes, treasury economist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday dollar/yen was revisiting levels last seen in August 1991. \par \par \uc2  Yen losses against the dollar and mark were sparked after a magazine reported U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin saying he was willing to let dollar/yen fall to 140 or even 150 if it was the only way to stop the Japanese economy collapsing. \par \par \uc2  Poor results from the Japanese banking sector is another factor behind the fall of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the mark and the dollar, Geddes said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese bank results for the financial year ending March 31 showed seven institutions suffering unrealised losses on their securities holdings. The holdings are a main source of funds for covering problem loan disposals. \par \par \uc2  At 1100 GMT the dollar was at 137.65/75 yen versus a session high of 137.75 yen and 137.10/20 yen on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Markets shrugged off warnings by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the "excessive weakness" of the yen could have the effect of boosting its trade surplus with the United States. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the mark is ranging and is seen supported by expectations of higher German interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment dollar/mark is pretty range-driven," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Barclays Capital. "There is not much new news." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the Bundesbank council meeting on Thursday would cause some jitters, even though expectations were for the German repo to be held at 3.30 percent. \par \par \uc2  Swiss franc sentiment had also made a recovery by the European midsession after the Swiss stock market recovered by more than one percent, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suffered a setback to sentiment after a report criticised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's wartime gold transactions. \par \par \uc2  The report said the Swiss National Bank knew as early as 1941 it was buying stolen gold from Nazi Germany. \par \par \uc2  At 1120 GMT the mark/Swiss franc was at 83/17.20 centimes versus a session high at 83.47. Dollar/Swiss franc was at 1.4716/21 versus 1.4715/25 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Australian dollar levels are expected to continue following the fortunes of the yen and commodity prices. \par \par \uc2  Foley at Barclays Capital expects to see the Australian dollar at 60 cents in the future. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at 62.25/30 cents versus 62.50/57 in Monday European trade. \par \par \uc2  ((Pratima Desai, London newsroom +44 171 542 2737)) \par \par \uc2  Treasuries prices, yields. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5q04ekt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
