\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr steady in late Tokyo as Indonesia calms. \b0\par\par\uc2 572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 1998\par 08:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  0618 GMT\tab     TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0600 GMT\par  USD/JPY     136.32/42\tab \tab 136.65-136.14\par  USD/DEM     1.7775/80\tab \tab 1.7812-1.7772\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 20 (Reuters) - The dollar was stuck in a tight \par \par \uc2  band against the yen on Wednesday due to apparently easing \par \par \uc2  concern about the situation in Indonesia, but caution persisted \par \par \uc2  and underpinned the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "The situation in Indonesia seems to be tranquil today and \par \par \uc2  no fresh riots were reported, but one can't tell," said a \par \par \uc2  senior dealer at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  "There is no light to break through the current situations in Indonesia as well as in Japan," said the bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "Given such conditions, the dollar is still in the process of testing 140 yen," he added. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the dollar slipped as low as 136.14 yen on news that Indonesian Moslem leader Amien Rais had called off plans for a mass "people power" demonstration against President Suharto. Rais later said he would join students occupying parliament, which helped support the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Rais told Reuters he expected 100,000 of his supporters to join him at the parliament complex. \par \par \uc2  "The Indonesian issue is of greatest concern to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, with many traders ready to sell yen if fresh riots or military action break out," said a trader at a U.S. bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Troops and tanks sealed off central Jakarta square where thousands of demonstrators had planned to gather on Wednesday to demand the ousting of Suharto. \par \par \uc2  Foreign Minister Keizo Obuchi said Japan is "extremely concerned" about the situation in Indonesia and voiced hopes that ongoing unrest there would not lead to bloodshed. \par \par \uc2  An Indonesian presidential spokesman said Suharto's new reform committee will be named on Thursday and its first priority is to set a schedule for fresh elections. But dealers expressed doubt about whether such steps would help soothe the angered masses in Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, dealers say the threat of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  intensifies whenever the dollar jumps rapidly. \par \par \uc2  "But a view that the BOJ will not step in to the market as long as the dollar/yen rate is stuck in a narrow range like today is prevailing," said a senior dealer at a European bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, declined to comment on Wednesday on markets or the situation in Indonesia. Asked about Indonesia and the reaction of financial markets, Sakakibara, told Reuters: "I have absolutely no comment." \par \par \uc2  The European bank dealer said: "Even if the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, its effect would be limited because of overwhelming demand for dollars from trust banks and other operators." Japanese exporters have sold dollars in the past two days, but their sales were not strong enough to halt the dollar's rise, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped below 1.7800 marks in Tokyo because of stop-loss dollar sales triggered at below 1.7790 marks following remarks by Bundesbank council member Ernst Welteke on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Welteke told a news conference at his regional \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Frankfurt: "I cannot rule out a slight interest rate hike, but it is too early to speculate when." \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 136.38/48 yen and 1.7834/39 marks in New York on Tuesday. ((Tatsuo Ito/Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5k04um8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
