\par\par \b \uc2  Yen Looks Set For Further Fall As Authorities Sidelined \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Brinsley \par \uc2 270 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 1998\par 13:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--What's a prudent \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do when faced with massive pressure for depreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a tottering domestic economy and little prospect of assistance from other central banks? \par \par \uc2  In Japan's case, beating a hasty retreat seems to be the response. \par \par \uc2  Japanese financial officials have continued to protest "excessive yen weakness" as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has tumbled in recent days. "We will act against an excessively weak yen in a serious and determined manner," Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga declared Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But in recent weeks such rhetoric hasn't been backed up by action - and that suggests Japanese authorities may be resigned to further yen depreciation, as long as it's not too fast, analysts and market particpants said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit an 80-month high of Y136.38 in New York on Monday, and hovered just below that level in Tokyo Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Its rise has brought it way past the Y133.50-level at which the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted massive dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early April, spending $15 billion to $20 billion of its foreign reserves, the world's largest. But since early April, no further BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been confirmed, despite some short-lived rumors. \par \par \uc2  Another sign of a more passive Japanese stance may be the recent silence of Eisuke Sakakibara, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs. Sakakibara, more than the finance minister or any other official, is viewed by the market as Japan's financial spokesman to the world. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 19-05-98 \par \par \uc2  1127GMT \par \par \uc2  1142 4205 62550 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5j049ne\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
