\par\par \b \uc2  Yen reprieve seen shortlived amid Indonesia crisis. \b0\par\par\uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 1998\par 09:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 19 (Reuters) - The yen started on a firmer note against the dollar in Europe, helped by a rebound in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked by Indonesian President Suharto's promise to call elections. \par \par \uc2  However, even though Suharto's promise not to seek re-election may have taken some of the heat out of mass protests planned in Indonesia on Wednesday, analysts said they took the news with a large pinch of salt. \par \par \uc2  "Suharto calls elections and has vowed not to stand himself - maybe pigs can fly after all?," said Mark Geddes, treasury economist at ABN AMRO. "Initially it will encourage profit-taking on short yen, Australian and New Zealand dollar positions built up recently. But there's no trend reversal." \par \par \uc2  Suharto's speech temporarily boosted the dollar as high as 136.30 in Asian trading after he said that if he quit he would not be seen as a responsible leader. But later remarks, signalling that he would not run for election again, spurred buying of yen and Asian units, as well as Japanse stocks. \par \par \uc2  As uncertainty about Indonesia remains and Japan's economic outlook continues gloomy, the yen will remain fragile, analysts said. The next critical upside target on dollar/yen is the monthly high from September 1991 at 136.88. \par \par \uc2  At 0725 GMT the dollar was at 135.70/80 yen, compared with 135.88/98 in late European trading on Monday and below the 136.40 peak hit yesterday. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also off lows against the mark, trading at 76.20/24 after falling to a five-year low at 76.35. Mark/yen was at 76.05/09 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was rangebound between 1.7800 and 1.7900 marks, trading at 1.7810/20 from 1.7874/84 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Concern about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was heightened after the Reserve Bank of Australia sold dollars to prop up its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga said on Tuesday Japan would act decisively against excessive yen weakness. He also said excessive weakness is not favourable. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is set to make gains if the yen continues to weaken on the back of Japanese economic weakness," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  "Continued talk that the BoJ could cut interest rates really undermines the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  argument and the two counter arguments are not helping yen at all." \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian crisis has weighed heavily on the Aussie dollar, dragging it to its lowest in 12 years at $0.6170 overnight. It rebounded sharply after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and steadied around $0.6250 in early Europe. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark remained in familiar ranges as dealers awaited the German Ifo Institute's business climate index for April. The west German index was expected to come in at 98.7 after 98.8 in March. \par \par \uc2  In other emerging markets, the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took action to protect the rouble on Tuesday with a sharp rise in official interest rates. It raised its refinancing rate to 50 percent from 30 percent. \par \par \uc2  The rouble, which is still not fully convertible into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was fixed slightly weaker at 6.1700 per dollar, from 6.1650 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling edged up against the mark and the dollar ahead of key UK retail price data, due at 0830 GMT. Analysts' forecasts point to a rise of 2.8 percent year-on-year in the underlying index, excluding mortgage payments, from 2.6 percent the previous month and well above the government's target of 2.5 percent. ((Astrid Zweynert, London newsroom, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 542 52 93, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5j04z6q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
