\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar falls in Tokyo as Suharto promises election. \b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 1998\par 08:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  0637 GMT\tab     TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0600 GMT\par  USD/JPY     135.65/75\tab \tab 136.30-135.63\par  USD/DEM     1.7827/32\tab \tab 1.7858-1.7825\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2      TOKYO, May 19 (Reuters) - The dollar fell from day's high \par \par \uc2  above 136 yen by late Tokyo on Tuesday as profit-taking \par \par \uc2  intensified, reflecting afternoon speech by Indonesian \par \par \uc2  President Suharto, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Suharto said that he promised new elections based on a new \par \par \uc2  law "as soon as possible" and also said he does not want to be \par \par \uc2  nominated to be president again. \par \par \uc2  Suharto's comments temporarily boosted the dollar as high as 136.30 yen after he said that if he quit he would not be seen as a responsible leader. But later remarks, signalling that he would not run for election again, spurred buying in the yen and Asian units, as well as Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  "Suharto's remarks clearly triggered profit-taking, mainly from overseas. But the extent of the dollar sales was limited as there were still many uncertainties about the outlook in Indonesia," a long-term credit bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "He commented on the election, but there was no clear time frame attached, so the situation is still not clear." \par \par \uc2  One senior dealer at a Japanese city bank said even though the market bought back the yen as Suharto promised to step down, the tension in Indonesia has not eased yet. \par \par \uc2  "There are still many uncertainties even if Suharto quits. I don't believe the market will continue buying the yen on Suharto's resignation," the city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said recoveries in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets encouraged some yen-buying, but aggressive follow-through action was limited due to the yen's weak fundamentals and technicals, especially after falling below this year's low of 135.45 yen against the dollar overnight. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar was well-supported around 135.60 yen by late trade amid bids from Japanese trust banks, short-covering and hedge-related buying. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the morning, the greenback was put under pressure as caution over possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased after Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars to prop up its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  The RBA confirmed it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday to buy the Australian dollar against the U.S. dollar. Dealers said earlier on Tuesday that the RBA had bought the Aussie at just over 62 cents after it touched 12-year low down around US$0.6177. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga said on Tuesday morning that Japan would act decisively against excessive weakness of the yen. He also said that the yen's excessive weakness is not favourable. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries are extremely strong in Tokyo, which is limiting the dollar's advance during (Tokyo) trading hours," another city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's advance should be limited in Tokyo, while the upward trend should continue to increase in overseas markets because of easing concerns over heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar will stay bullish against the yen, but a wait-and-see mood could emerge before the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) policy meeting later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The market is not expecting the Fed to change its credit policy at the FOMC meeting. Dealers are also closely watching Thursday's release of the March 31 FOMC minutes. \par \par \uc2  A large-scale student protest meeting is expected in Indonesia on Wednesday. A Moslem leader said on Tuesday that planned protests would go ahead on Wednesday and he hoped the rallies would be peaceful, but he feared violence. ((Chikafumi Hodo, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5j04yx9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
