\par\par \b \uc2  Yen bears prowl, feeding on Asian turmoil. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 1998\par 12:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 14 (Reuters) - Yen bears were on the prowl on Thursday, forcing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closer to the precipice as mounting concern about Asia's economic and political woes overshadowed wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Escalating tensions in Indonesia were the trigger for the latest bout of selling which drove the yen to one-month lows against the dollar and 18-month lows against the mark. \par \par \uc2  With Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to tumble further and Japan's economy already at risk of tipping back into recession, traders are gearing up to test central banks' resolve to defend the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The picture in Southeast Asia is turning out to be grimmer than people had thought it was going to be and we sense that a climate of competitive devaluations is in the making," said Mike Lenhoff, portfolio strategist at Capel-Cure Myers Capital Management in London. \par \par \uc2  "The yen is part of that picture and we are looking for it to go to 140 against the dollar." \par \par \uc2  By 1008 GMT, the yen had already depreciated as far as 134.58 per dollar, its weakest since April 7, and hit 75.62 per mark, its worst showing since November 1996. Its losses came as the Indonesian rupiah sank to two-month lows against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  While traders are now gunning for the 135 barrier on dollar/yen, they noted the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before leaders of the Group of Eight nations met on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, a senior Japanese Finance Ministry official, kept the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alive after he said on Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take appropriate measures in response to the excessive weakness of the yen and that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would eventually affect the market. \par \par \uc2  "A move above 135 would spell \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Martin de Blocq, sales manager at Nomura. He said he believed the U.S. could join the Bank of Japan in mounting a defence of the yen. \par \par \uc2  Still, he added that only a minority of traders were looking for such co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has not been seen since 1995. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Asia's woes would deter the U.S. from taking part in a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which could prove ineffectual. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not make a lot of sense during the Asian maelstrom," said Kit Juckes, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at NatWest Global Financial Markets in London, who sees the dollar rising toward 136 yen in the coming days. \par \par \uc2  The weakness of Japan's economy also risks undercutting the impact of any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yen's defence. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's Nihon Keizai Shimbun paper said a senior Bank of Japan official acknowledged deflation could affect the Japanese economy by the end of summer but added that the probability of a deflationary spiral was low, according to Nikkei English news. \par \par \uc2  "Allowing the yen to depreciate slowly would be part of creating an expansionary environment and part and parcel of what is required to get the economy going," said Lenhoff at Capel-Cure Myers. \par \par \uc2  Against this backdrop, Brian Martin, chief economist at Barclays Capital, expects the dollar to rally to 137.50 by the end of next week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 134.16/26 yen at 1024 GMT from 133.98/03 late in Europe on Wednesday. Mark/yen was at 75.52/55 from 75.34/40 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, dollar/mark was largely sidelined, content to take its cue from losses in sterling/mark. The dollar was at 1.7762/67 marks from 1.7770/82 late in Europe on Wednesday while the pound was at 2.8797/07 from 2.9015/25 yesterday. \par \par \uc2  The Bundesbank was expected to leave interest rates unchanged at its policy meeting on Thursday but direction could come from the speech council member Reimut Jochimsen is to deliver 1430 GMT. \par \par \uc2  U.S. April consumer prices and real earnings data are released at 1230 GMT. ((Swaha Pattanaik, London newsroom, +44 171 542 6284, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  Treasuries prices, yields. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5e05ewd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
