\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Midday: Dlr Falls Back Slightly As Resistance Holds \b0\par\par\uc2 380 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 1998\par 04:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar slipped lower versus the yen after failing to breach a key resistance level, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slightly lower at midday in Tokyo Wednesday compared with its levels earlier in the session. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar continues to hover near the Y134 mark, as an absence of yen-supportive comments from Vice Minister of Finance for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara earlier in the day and weak sentiment surrounding Japan's economy support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar ran into resistance around Y134.15 after touching an intraday high of Y134.14 at 0143 GMT. It was the dollar's highest level versus the yen since January 7, when the dollar hit Y134.40. \par \par \uc2  "There's no question the yen is heading down rather than up," said Minoru Shioiri, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nippon Credit Bank. "But it's not easy to chase the dollar higher," he said, citing defensive dollar-selling interest to protect options with strike prices around Y134.50 and caution against the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara told reporters earlier that the sharp rise in the country's current account surplus was partly due to the weak yen, and the U.S. understands that. \par \par \uc2  But he declined to comment on the dollar-yen, prompting some players to buy the dollar against the yen earlier, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's current account surplus for March before adjustment for seasonal factors jumped 43.8% from the year-earlier month to Y1.496 trillion, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. The figure was below the Y1.623 trillion forecast by Tokyo-based economists surveyed by Dow Jones. \par \par \uc2  Around 0230 GMT (10:30 p.m. EDT), the dollar was trading at Y133.95, down from Y134.00 around 0015 GMT (8:15 p.m. EDT) and unchanged from Y133.95 in New York late Tuesday. The dollar was quoted at Y133.06 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also trading at DEM1.1.7765, just below DEM1.7766 earlier in Tokyo and above DEM1.7755 late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was at DEM1.7764 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 13-05-98 \par \par \uc2  0232GMT \par \par \uc2  1074 1863 4162 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5d03zg1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
