\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr slides, led by losses vs resurgent Swiss franc. \b0\par\par\uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 7 (Reuters) - The dollar fell across the board by midsession on Thursday, its slide to three-month lows against the mark led by losses it suffered against the Swiss franc amid demand for a safe haven from renewed turmoil in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide to a two-month low of 1.4650 Swiss francs undermined it against the yen. Dollar/yen also ceded ground from four-week peaks hit overnight as traders speculated renewed pressure on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could prompt Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We've seen a lot of buying in the Swiss on the back of the Asian troubles," a trader at a British bank in London said. \par \par \uc2  Nervousness about growing instability in Indonesia, where fresh rioting broke out on Wednesday, has propelled regional shares and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. \par \par \uc2  The yen fell as low as 75.59 marks, its worst showing since October 30, 1996, and hit 133.60 against the dollar overnight. However, wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  halted its slide and helped it to pare its losses, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "If Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to go pear-shaped, then that increases the chances not just of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  but of other G7 countries coming in to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns. \par \par \uc2  London-based insurers decided to raise the cost of some insurance in Indonesia due to the recent riots and demonstrations. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hovered above its lows against the Swiss franc at 1129 GMT, trading at 1.4680/90 compared with 1.4735/45 late in Europe on Wednesday. Dollar/mark was at 1.7626/36 from a session low of 1.7606 and 1.7653/58 late in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was near session lows, trading at 132.73/83 from an intraday high of 133.60 and 132.70/75 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the dollar would renew its move higher against the yen amid continuing gloom over the country's economy. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar/yen will hit 145 ultimately," Barrow said. \par \par \uc2  "Only when the economy is back on track again can there be better prospects for the yen to strengthen." \par \par \uc2  Comments from Japanese officials have had no effect on the widespread bearish sentiment for dollar/yen, he said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister Eisuke Sakakibara told economists in London last week that Japanese data in coming months would be gloomy but a recovery was a priority. \par \par \uc2  Analysts are less optimistic about dollar/mark's prospects and said it would be difficult for the dollar to gain versus the mark against the backdrop of persistent speculation of a German interest rate rise. \par \par \uc2  Michael Derks, senior strategist at Nomura Research International in London, said the mark was being boosted by investor interest in the euro, which will be launched in 1999. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen was firmer but had eased off the 18-month high struck earlier. The mark was at 75.29/33 by 1158 GMT from 75.16/21 in late trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling hit a 4-1/2-month low of 2.9110 marks but pared losses after the Bank of England left rates unchanged, in line with expectations. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank council member Hans-Juergen Krupp attends a discussion on monetary policy before EMU at 1430 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also eyeing the speech that Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan is to deliver at 1600 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Fed Vice Chair Alice Rivlin is due to speak at 1325 GMT and again at 1900 GMT. The Federal Reserve Bank of New York holds its quarterly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefing at 1430 GMT while Assistant Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner speaks on the euro and the dollar at 1630 GMT. \par \par \uc2  U.S. weekly jobless claims are released at 1230 GMT. U.S. first-quarter productivity and labour cost data and March wholesale trade report are out at 1400 GMT. ((Elizabeth Piper, London newsroom, +44 171 542 6284, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5705xnw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
