\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar well-bid at 133 yen in mid-morning Tokyo. \b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      TOKYO, May 7 (Reuters) - ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACTIVITY: \par \par \uc2      0122 GMT --*Dollar well-bid just above 133 yen in \par \par \uc2  mid-morning trade due to solid bids from various operators amid \par \par \uc2  renewed concern over economies of Asian nations, including \par \par \uc2  Japan. *Dollar standing at 133.10/20 yen at 0123 GMT. \par \par \uc2      *Options-related sales seen near 133.50 yen, but stop-loss \par \par \uc2  buying also expected once that point pierced, traders said. \par \par \uc2      *"There is a talk of options-related positions at 133.50 \par \par \uc2  yen, but that would not be a major obstacle for the dollar's \par \par \uc2  rise," said a dealer at a Japanese bank. "A clear break of that \par \par \uc2  point may send the dollar up to 133.60-70 yen," he added. \par \par \uc2  *Dealers said bids stemmed from short-term speculators, trust banks and foreign operators. \par \par \uc2  *"Renewed worries about Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , coupled with weak Japanese stocks and falling Japanese bond yields, won't work in favour of yen buying," said the Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  *Indonesia rupiah fell sharply vs dollar on riots in north Sumatra city of Medan and several other cities. \par \par \uc2  *Rupiah quoted at 9700/10,200 against dollar at 0100 GMT. \par \par \uc2  *Concerns over possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lingering, but one trader said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not step into market until dollar reaches 135 yen. \par \par \uc2  0022 GMT \par \par \uc2  *Dollar steady and keeping firm undertone against yen in nervous early Tokyo on Thursday as yen stayed under selling pressure amid worries over Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japanese share prices, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Interbank operators dominated activity in light early trade, with strong bargain-hunting demand protecting dollar's downside below 133.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  *Knockout options rumoured placed at around 133.50 yen and dollar-sales aimed at protecting options may emerge should dollar approach that level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Selling interest from Japanese exporters expected above 133.50 yen, which should make operators cautious about testing above that level. \par \par \uc2  *Some dealers concerned about comments on yuan by chief economist of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dealers said they want to see how comments will affect Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  *Yi Gang, chief economist of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said yuan will remain at current level against dollar as promised by government. But he said in an academic paper given in Australia that pressure would increase in second half of 1998 for devaluation of yuan. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5705wxu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
