\par\par \b \uc2  Sakakibara Says  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  April  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Defended Yen Target Level \b0\par\par\uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 1998\par 18:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- Eisuke Sakakibara, the influential vice minister for international affairs at the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has indicated that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at preventing the yen falling through a key "threshold level." \par \par \uc2  Leading London economists who attended a private session with the official last Friday said he didn't specify a particular level, but some came away with the impression that it is around 135.00 yen to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The threshold level is the point at which the Bank of Japan might be seen losing control of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , one of the economists said. \par \par \uc2  The economists said that when asked what \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 'threshold level' is, Sakakibara replied: 'We're \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so you work it out.' \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the yen just before the Easter holidays and several days before a Group of Seven meeting in Washington when the dollar had started to turn down from a seven-year high at Y135.43 on April 3. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is estimated to have spent $18 billion, leaving itself with just over $200 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Although it dipped under 130.00 yen briefly since then, the dollar has since rebounded to 132.25 yen late Wednesday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  The eight economists were invited to hear Sakakibara give a robust defense of the Japanese economy and then partook in what was described as a quite aggressive question and answer session. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara is said to have warned that economic 'numbers for the next month or two will be quite gloomy.' \par \par \uc2  Although the real economy is seen having bottomed out in March or April, he expects bad numbers over the next two months. \par \par \uc2  As a result, he sees the next Bank of Japan survey of business sentiment, known as the Tankan Report, bringing further bad news about the Japanese economy when it is published in July. But he expects the recent series of economic stimulation packages to start helping to lift the economy by the time the next Tankan is published in October. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara is said to have stressed that ensuring economic recovery is key at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that plans for fiscal consolidation have been put on the back burner for the time being. \par \par \uc2  "Fiscal consolidation can wait, recovery is the key priority," one of the economists reported him saying. \par \par \uc2  When asked about U.S. attitudes to the dollar's strength, Sakakibara told the economists that U.S. officials are concerned about the U.S. growing trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  Many expect a deterioration in the deficit later this year to raise trade tensions between the U.S. and Japan and put pressure on the U.S. to allow the dollar to fall. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du5600qq3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
