\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar downed in U.S. by talk of Euro rate hikes. \b0\par\par\uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 5 (Reuters) - The dollar tumbled against the mark and yen on Tuesday as dealers predicted a wave of European rate hikes before next year's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  union and investors took profits on yen positions. \par \par \uc2  Trading volumes picked up on Tuesday as London dealers returned from Monday's bank holiday and should return to normal levels when Tokyo trading resumes overnight after holidays Monday and Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said controversy about the weekend's hard-fought deal to appoint the head of Europe's future \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lingered and helped spark more talk of European rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1.7690/00 marks after opening at 1.7708/13 and stood at 131.58/68 yen, below the 132.50/60 opening level. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said critical comments about an 11th-hour deal appointing Dutchman Wim Duisenberg to head the ECB for the first four years stoked talk European rates may soon be raised in a bid to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer said "Not everything that happened last weekend in Brussels contributed to the necessary expectation that the euro will be a really supranational and depoliticised \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Denmark surprised markets by raising its discount and repo rates by half a percentage point to support its ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and help set the speculation into motion dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Talk the Germans might be ready to raise their repo rate this summer has given the dollar extra lift," explained Greg Schwake, trader at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  While Bundesbank members, including directorate member Edgar Meister, insisted the Brussels decision would have no direct impact on German interest rates, traders were not convinced. "I can't believe Germany will just sit there and not do anything to rates while everyone else cuts," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Additionally, dealers said there was some talk of selling of dollar reserves as European nations will soon be able to pare back these holdings. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's drop appeared to pave the way for a decline towards 1.75 marks, traders speculated, even though they said the Bundesbank would likely not be happy about such sharp dollar movements. \par \par \uc2  "I could see some verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or maybe they will just continue to talk about the fact they won't do anything on interest rates as a means to slow the dollar's drop," Schwake added. \par \par \uc2  In trading against the yen, dealers said there was some profit-taking on short yen positions, particularly as the market grew more nervous about the potential of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin warned Tokyo against using a weak yen to jump-start economic growth. In a television interview, Rubin said, "For Japan's own sake and for the sake of the rest of the world ... what they need to do is not to depend on the yen falling to generate more export-led growth, but just the opposite. What they need do to is generate domestic demand-led growth." \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the British pound ended essentially flat at $1.6615/25 from $1.6618/23 at the open. Dealers had said sterling's luster could dull now as economic growth appears to be slowing, eliminating the need to raise rates again. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to 1.4800/10 Swiss francs from 1.4818/28 at the open and nudged slightly higher against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4390/95 from C$1.4371/76. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar ended at $0.6389/94 down from $0.6402/07. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1638 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du55061i2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
