\par\par \b \uc2  Costly  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  By  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Pushed  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Reserves Down By 8% \b0\par\par\uc2  By Brian Fowler and Arran Scott, Staff Reporters \par \uc2 854 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 1998\par 12:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- If there were any doubts about how far Japan is willing to go to support the yen, they were dispelled by the release Friday of data showing a deep decline in Japan's foreign reserves last month. \par \par \uc2  But the numbers also underscored the price of efforts to keep the yen from going into a free-fall. The fact that massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities shielded the yen's value only fleetingly suggests that economic fundamentals will continue to prevail over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that the value of Japan's reserves fell $17.84 billion in April, the largest-ever monthly drop, to $205.76 billion. The ministry typically doesn't offer any explanations about why the level of foreign reserves changes in any given month, but this time a ministry official acknowledged that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen was the main factor behind the decline. \par \par \uc2  Given that Japanese foreign reserves usually expand by between $1 billion and $2 billion every month due to interest accrued on holdings, the latest figures imply that the Band of Japan spent as much as $20 billion in its defense of the yen last month. To put the scale of that action in perspective, Canada's total reserves stood at $22.42 billion as of the end of March and Australia's official reserves came to $17.25 billion as of the end of February. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the figures proved that the ministry is willing to put its money where its mouth is, backing up its verbal warnings about excessive yen weakness with outright action. \par \par \uc2  But they said the figures illustrate how difficult the ministry's task has been and will remain. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-April began when the dollar was trading between 133.40 yen and 133.60 yen. Although the dollar-selling helped push the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as low as 127.40 yen on April 10, the dollar later bounced back. Late Friday in Asia, the dollar was quoted around 133.20 yen, up from 132.83 yen late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  "I think the most important fact to come out of this data is that despite spending around $20 billion to defend the yen, the (Bank of Japan) couldn't keep the dollar from coming back," said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in Tokyo. "The fundamental case for dollar bullishness is so strong that even $20 billion in selling couldn't hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down." \par \par \uc2  Analysts cited the stark contrast between the U.S. and Japanese economies as the main reason for the dollar's unabated buoyancy. That gulf was highlighted this week by economic data released by the two countries. The figures showed that U.S. gross domestic product continued to grow at a robust pace in the first quarter of 1998, rising by 4.2% from the prior quarter, even as inflation remained in check. In Japan, on the other hand, industrial output continued to shrivel and the jobless rate surged to a new record high 3.9% in March. \par \par \uc2  "We are revising our 1998 year-end \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast (for the yen) downward to put the dollar at 145 yen due to the weak performance of Japanese assets and depressed net demand for Japanese goods and services," Andrew Shipley, economist at Schroders Securities in Tokyo, said. \par \par \uc2  Others said, however, that although the dollar hasn't fallen much despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar-selling was successful in putting a floor under the yen - at least for now. "You can't say that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was entirely ineffective," said Ryohei Muramatsu, treasury manager at Commerzbank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar likely would be at 140 yen already, thereby fueling the so-called "sell Japan" phenomenon that Japanese authorities fear. \par \par \uc2  Muramatsu noted that the large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month put two-way risk into the market that helped stabilize the dollar-yen rate. \par \par \uc2  But despite the lingering concerns about further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar's upward trend hasn't been broken, most analysts said. They said as long as U.S. economic growth remains steady, Washington policy makers aren't likely to risk upsetting the financial markets by undermining the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The ministry official said that judging from the most recent statistics available in each country, Japan continues to hold the largest reserves of any nation. At the end of April, Japan's reserves stood at $205.76 billion. Next was China with $144.3 billion at Jan. 31, followed by Taiwan, which had $84.7 billion on the same date. 31. Data for China and Taiwan tend to lag those in other countries. Germany was fourth with $82.2 billion as of Feb. 28 while Hong Kong was fifth with $80.3 billion at Jan. 31 \par \par \uc2  -Brian Fowler and Arran Scott; 813-5255-2929 \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  1146 4205 62558 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du5100r53\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
