\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr drifting narrowly in Tokyo before long holiday. \b0\par\par\uc2 636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  0621 GMT\tab     TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0600 GMT\par  USD/JPY     133.07/13\tab \tab 133.19-132.67\par  USD/DEM     1.7934/39\tab \tab 1.7963-1.7912\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2      TOKYO, May 1 (Reuters) - The dollar was stuck at around 133 \par \par \uc2  yen in becalmed late trade on Friday, with most operators \par \par \uc2  halting activity ahead of Japanese national holidays. \par \par \uc2      The greenback briefly rose to 133.19 yen, its highest level \par \par \uc2  since the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the New York \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Bank to sell dollars in New York on April 9. \par \par \uc2  But it was then capped by sales by exporters and profit-takers. \par \par \uc2  "There was virtually nothing going on in afternoon trade, with many operators gearing up for a long holiday," said a trader at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's financial markets will be closed next Monday and Tuesday for national holidays. \par \par \uc2  Some traders speculated that the dollar may rise to 134 yen or 135 yen while the Tokyo markets are closed, but others remained cautious about BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overseas whenever the dollar rises excessively. But the Japanese bank dealer said: "I don't think the BOJ will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in overseas markets) while Tokyo operators are away on holidays." \par \par \uc2  The Japanese bank dealer said although many Tokyo operators were reluctant to start buying dollars at the 133 yen level, overseas funds who appeared to have completed position adjustments may be able to take fresh dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  In morning trade, the yen came under pressure due to disappointment over comments made by Taku Yamasaki, policy chief of Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP), during a U.S. visit. Yamasaki told reporters in Washington on Thursday that Japan was considering tax system changes from a medium-to long-term perspective. One dealer said such slow debate on the tax system could not help the economy. \par \par \uc2  Yamasaki also said Japan's economic stimulus package had won praise from U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin and Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  Falling Japanese government bond (JGB) yields and weak Tokyo stock prices weighed on the yen in morning trade. \par \par \uc2  The 182nd 10-year JGB yield was a record low 1.435 percent at 0645 GMT, and the Nikkei 225 average ended down 0.26 percent at 15,601.10. It was in negative territory most of the day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Japanese investor demand for dollars relating to their fresh foreign securities investment was strong amid a poor investment climate at home. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a European bank in Tokyo said the fact that the mark topped its previous high of 74.15 yen, set last December, fuelled a bullish tone for the dollar/yen rate. \par \par \uc2  The mark stood at around 74.24/27 yen at 0658 GMT, compared with around 73.98 yen in New York late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they would focus on a three-day European Union summit starting later on Friday that is to decide which countries join the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the outset. \par \par \uc2  "Regarding European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade, many traders were taking a wait-and-see attitude in the afternoon before the summit," said a dealer at another European bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  A continued dispute over who should head Europe's future \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was making some Tokyo traders nervous. \par \par \uc2  Traders focused on how the dispute might be settled and how it would affect the mark. France has nominated Jean-Claude Trichet, governor of the Bank of France, for the job and recently threatened to veto any decision it opposes. \par \par \uc2  Most other EU nations favour Dutchman Wim Duisenberg, the president of the European Monetary Institute. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 132.80/90 yen and 1.7954/59 marks in New York on Thursday. ((Tatsuo Ito 81-3-3432-1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5106bf3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
