\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr ends US flat to lower, mark up as EMU nears. \b0\par\par\uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 1 (Reuters) - The dollar fell nearly a pfennig against the mark on Friday amid optimism over Europe's planned monetary union and speculation that German rates would head higher later this year, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, dollar/yen was flat, shaking off rumors the Federal Reserve had checked \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and supported as mark/yen surged to a 15-month high near 75. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said sales of the dollar, British pound and Swiss franc for marks accelerated on Friday as the market's perceived need to hold safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of Europe's planned monetary union faded. \par \par \uc2  The January 1, 1999 launch of the new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro, in 11 of 15 European Union (EU) member states is expected to be approved by EU leaders at a weekend summit in Brussels. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a political row over who will head Europe's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a wildcard for the summit and market confidence in the Euro. But several finance ministers on Friday said they were optimistic the dispute would be settled. \par \par \uc2  "People are unwinding a lot of those positions where they were looking for safe haven status if the euro didn't go off ... but euro's a done deal, it's over," said John Gariano, vice president at Sumitomo Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Financial market closures in much of Europe for the May Day holiday kept activity thin, which exacerbated moves caused by "excitement over the euro" and unwinding trades, said a dealer at a U.S. investment bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar bounced from a three-month low of 1.7735 marks, but still ended off at 1.7800/10 from 1.7895/05 at the open. It was unchange at 133.28/38 yen. \par \par \uc2  Market speculation that the new European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) would start its key short-term lending rate near four percent - above Germany's 3.3 percent repo rate - also sparked buying of marks against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "There is speculation the Bundesbank will raise rates in order to meet the EMU convergence and that there might be some sort of announcement coming out of the meetings this weekend," said Nicholas Neuheimer, an analyst at WEFA Inc. \par \par \uc2  Neuheimer noted that a risk of weekend meetings could be that EU leaders hint at a lower convergence rate below four percent, and necessitate countries like Italy and Spain to cut their relatively high rates further. \par \par \uc2  The analyst still expected the ECB's initial short-term rate would be above Germany's current rates, perhaps at 3.7 percent or 3.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank officials in recent days downplayed chances for a German rate increase, including council member Klaus-Dieter Kuehbacher who said he saw no sensible reason for a 1998 hike. \par \par \uc2  "You could interpret that as saying there's no domestic reason because there is no trace of inflation in the German economy. However, if they are committed to having low inflation in the rest of Europe it's in their interest to raise interest rates," Neuheimer said. \par \par \uc2  In dollar/yen, fears of possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair lower but the talk was soon ascribed to routine Fed \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and not the precursor to dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  Rumors swirled that the Federal Reserve was checking rates via an electronic broker system, rattling nerves on edge since the massive dollar sales placed through the Fed by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on April 9 and 10. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Japan's weak economy and financial system continue to dictate a weaker yen. On Friday, U.S. rating agency Standard & Poor's told Reuters that government stimulus measures have not been sufficient to help Japanese banks and the credit ratings would remain under pressure for some time. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar fell to 1.4915/25 Swiss francs from 1.4980/90 at the open and sterling dipped to $1.6662/72 from $1.6675/85. The dollar edged up to Canadian $1.4338/43 from C$1.4306/11 and the Australian dollar was lower at $0.6483/88 from $0.6515/20., ((-Al Yoon, 212-859-1672)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du51063qt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
